const DECKS = {};
DECKS.art = {
  title:'The Art of Rhetoric', sub:'The definition and the parts of the art',
  sources:['arist_rhet','arist_freese','cic_inv','ad_her','cic_orat','quint','ddc','cic_yonge','plato_jowett'],
  panels:[
{ id:'why-art', src:['arist_rhet','plato_jowett','appown'], pids:['plato-ap-1'],
  h:'<p><strong>Why is persuasion an art?</strong></p><p>Since we can do the same thing well or badly, and since we can teach both the reason why one person is able to persuade another and also why someone else may try to persuade but fail, persuasion is not only a natural gift but also an art.</p><p>Aristotle gives this reason at the opening of the <em>Rhetoric</em>. Everyone, he says, tries up to a point to defend an argument or to attack one, and ordinary people “do this either at random or through practice and from acquired habit. Both ways being possible, the subject can plainly be handled systematically, for it is possible to inquire the reason why some speakers succeed through practice and others spontaneously; and every one will at once agree that such an inquiry is the function of an art” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.1).</p><p>Rhetoric is the third art of the classical trivium (which is the coordinated studies of grammar, logic, and rhetoric), and each of the three arts asks a different question about speech. Grammar asks whether the utterance is well formed, that is, whether its parts fit together (<span class="lat">congruitas</span>). Logic asks whether what is said is true, and what follows from it. Rhetoric asks whether the hearer is moved to see it. The three questions are distinct: “The moon is made of green cheese” is well formed and false, whereas “Him go store yesterday” is not well formed and may well be true. A speech may also be well formed and true and still fail to persuade, because it does not find what will move these hearers in this case. Socrates says of his accusers that they spoke so persuasively that he almost forgot who he was, “and yet they have hardly uttered a word of truth” (Plato, <em>Apology</em> 17a). So persuasion and truth are not the same thing, and the art is needed because what is true does not always persuade.</p><p>To say that persuasion is an art, however, is not yet to say what the art is.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why does Aristotle hold that persuasion can be treated as an art?',
      options:['Because everyone persuades well by nature, so practice adds nothing','Because the reason why some speakers succeed can be found and taught','Because persuasion is the same as proof, and proof can always be taught','Because only trained speakers persuade, and ordinary people never do'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Aristotle observes that people persuade at random or by habit, and that the reason why some succeed can be inquired into; such an inquiry is the work of an art. He does not say that ordinary people never persuade, and he does not make persuasion the same as proof.' } },
{ id:'definition', src:['arist_rhet','quint'],
  h:'<p><strong>What is the definition of rhetoric?</strong></p><p>A doctor persuades us about our health, and an engineer persuades us that a bridge will or will not hold; each persuades within his own subject. But a lawyer before a jury, a member of a town council, or a speaker at a funeral may have to speak about almost any subject, and what such a speaker needs is not one more body of knowledge but the ability to see, in the case before him, what will persuade these hearers.</p><p>Aristotle defines the art in just this way: “Rhetoric may be defined as the faculty of observing in any given case the available means of persuasion” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.2). He adds that this “is not a function of any other art,” because every other art persuades only about its own subject, as medicine does about health. The definition does not say that rhetoric is the art of winning. Aristotle compares it to medicine: “it is not the function of medicine simply to make a man quite healthy, but to put him as far as may be on the road to health.” In the same way, the work of rhetoric “is not simply to succeed in persuading, but rather to discover the means of coming as near such success as the circumstances of each particular case allow” (I.1). A speaker who has seen everything that could be said may still lose, and his art has not failed.</p><p>Aristotle also calls rhetoric “the counterpart of Dialectic” (I.1). Dialectic is the art of arguing by question and answer from opinions that are generally held. The two arts are alike in that neither belongs to a special science; both deal with matters that all people can discuss. But dialectic argues with one partner, step by step, whereas rhetoric addresses a whole body of hearers, who cannot follow a long chain of reasoning.</p><p>Quintilian gives the Roman definition: rhetoric is “the science of speaking well” (<span class="lat">bene dicendi scientia</span>; <em>Institutio oratoria</em> II.15.34). He prefers it because it includes the character of the orator, “since no man can speak well who is not good himself.” The question of character matters because the art can serve falsehood as well as truth. Aristotle grants that a man who uses the power of speech unjustly can do great harm, but he answers that this “is a charge which may be made in common against all good things except virtue” (I.1), such as strength, health, and wealth. So it matters who learns the art and for what end.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker brings every argument that the case allows and still loses the vote. On Aristotle’s account, has he failed in the art?',
      options:['Yes, because the end of the art is to win, so a lost vote shows a lack of art','No, because no speech can ever fail in the art, whatever the speaker does','No, because the art is to see the available means of persuasion, not always to win','Yes, because a speaker who has the art persuades every hearer in every case'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Aristotle defines rhetoric as the faculty of observing the available means of persuasion, and he says that its work is to come as near to success as the case allows, as medicine puts a patient as far as may be on the road to health. A speech can fail in the art, but losing is not the same thing as failing in it.' } },
{ id:'five-parts', src:['cic_inv','arist_freese','appown'],
  h:'<p><strong>The five parts of the art, in order</strong></p><p>Someone asked to give a toast at a wedding has several different tasks. First he must decide what to say about the couple: which stories to tell, what to praise, and what to wish them. Then he must decide in what order to say it, and in what words. Then he must remember it, so that he is not reading from a page, and finally he must say it aloud, with a voice and a manner that suit the occasion. A speaker can do any one of these well and another badly, so each is a distinct part of the work.</p><p>The classical tradition names the same five parts. Cicero lists them as “Invention; Arrangement; Elocution; Memory; Delivery” (<em>De inventione</em> I.7.9):</p><p><strong>Invention</strong> (<span class="lat">inventio</span>): finding what to say, that is, the arguments and the other means of persuasion; Cicero calls it “the conceiving of topics either true or probable, which may make one’s cause appear probable.”<br><strong>Arrangement</strong> (<span class="lat">dispositio</span>): putting what has been found into a fitting order.<br><strong>Style</strong> (<span class="lat">elocutio</span>): choosing and arranging the words. (The older English name, “elocution,” here means style, not the art of speaking aloud.)<br><strong>Memory</strong> (<span class="lat">memoria</span>): holding the matter and the words in mind.<br><strong>Delivery</strong> (<span class="lat">pronuntiatio</span> or <span class="lat">actio</span>): the use of the voice and the body in speaking.</p><p>The order is not arbitrary. A speaker who has found nothing to say has nothing to arrange, and he has no use for words until he knows what they are to say; so invention comes first and takes the most space. Aristotle states the same order at the beginning of the third book of the <em>Rhetoric</em>: first “what gives things themselves their persuasiveness,” then style, and then delivery (III.1.3).</p><p>This course follows that order. Invention is studied in Divisions II and III (the arguments, and then the character of the speaker and the passions of the hearers), arrangement in Division IV, style and the figures in Divisions V and VI, and in Division VII all the parts together in whole cases. Memory and delivery are treated here only briefly, and we consider them next.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker has strong arguments set in a good order, but he reads them in a flat voice, with no change of tone where the speech should move the hearers. In which part of the art has he failed?',
      options:['Arrangement','Invention','Delivery','Style'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Delivery is the use of the voice and the body in speaking. His arguments (invention) and their order (arrangement) are good, and nothing is said against his words (style); what fails is the voice.' } },
{ id:'memory-delivery', src:['arist_freese','cic_inv','ad_her','cic_orat','quint'],
  h:'<p><strong>Memory and delivery</strong></p><p>A good speech read badly, in a flat voice and with the eyes on the page, can fail where a weaker speech said well succeeds. A speaker who must read every word cannot look at his hearers, and he cannot notice when they are puzzled or restless and answer what happens in the room. So memory and delivery are true parts of the art, although they are learned chiefly by practice, by speaking aloud and by learning good passages by heart, more than by precept.</p><p>Cicero defines <strong>memory</strong> as “the lasting sense in the mind of the matters and words corresponding to the reception of these topics,” and <strong>delivery</strong> as “a regulating of the voice and body in a manner suitable to the dignity of the subjects spoken of and of the language employed” (<em>De inventione</em> I.7.9). That is, the speaker must keep in mind both what he means to say and how he means to say it, and his voice and bearing must fit both the subject and the words.</p><p>Someone who must remember a list of errands may picture walking through his own house and leaving each errand in a room: the letter to be posted on the doorstep, the bread to be bought on the kitchen table, the call to be made beside the stairs. To recall the list, he walks through the house again in his mind. The <em>Rhetorica ad Herennium</em>, a Latin handbook of the first century BC long attributed to Cicero, teaches a trained memory of this kind. It distinguishes natural memory, which is born with us, from artificial memory, which training strengthens, and it says that the artificial memory consists of places and images (<span class="lat">constat igitur artificiosa memoria locis et imaginibus</span>; III.16.28–29). The places are settings that are easy to hold in mind, such as a house, the space between two columns, a corner, or an arch, and they are kept in a fixed order, so that the speaker can begin at any point and go forward or back. The images are likenesses of the things to be remembered, set in those places, and the handbook adds that striking images, very beautiful or very ugly or strange, are remembered better than ordinary ones (III.22.35–37). Cicero, who credits the poet Simonides of Ceos with having “first invented an art of memory,” puts the principle in one sentence: “thus the order of places would preserve the order of things, and the symbols of the things would denote the things themselves; so that we should use the places as waxen tablets, and the symbols as letters” (<em>De oratore</em> II.351–354). Quintilian shows how a speaker lays out a speech in a house: “The first thought is placed, as it were, in the forecourt; the second, let us say, in the living-room; the remainder are placed in due order all round the impluvium” (<em>Institutio oratoria</em> XI.2.20), that is, round the open court at the center of a Roman house.</p><p>Delivery, Cicero says, is “a sort of eloquence of the body, consisting as it does of voice and motion” (<em>Orator</em> 55), and he gives it the highest place: “Delivery, I say, has the sole and supreme power in oratory; without it, a speaker of the highest mental capacity can be held in no esteem; while one of moderate abilities, with this qualification, may surpass even those of the highest talent” (<em>De oratore</em> III.213). Demosthenes, asked what came first in eloquence, is said to have answered delivery, and the same for second and third. Quintilian agrees that “a mediocre speech supported by all the power of delivery will be more impressive than the best speech unaccompanied by such power” (XI.3.5).</p><p>Aristotle says that delivery “is of the greatest importance but has not yet been treated of by anyone,” and that it “is a matter of voice, as to the mode in which it should be used for each particular emotion; when it should be loud, when low, when intermediate” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.1.3–4). He is not pleased that it counts for so much. In justice, he says, a case should be fought “with the facts alone”; but because the hearers are as they are, “we must pay attention to it, not as being right, but necessary” (III.1.5). Delivery, then, does not replace argument, but a speaker who neglects it may lose a case that his arguments deserved to win.</p><p>Memory and delivery depend on the other parts and come last, and they are learned more by practice than by rules. That raises the question how the art as a whole is learned.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why, on Aristotle’s account, must the speaker attend to delivery?',
      options:['Because hearers are moved by it, so it is necessary, though not right in itself','Because delivery is the noblest part of the art and so should be studied before invention','Because a speech that is delivered well needs no arguments at all','Because the voice shows whether what the speaker says is true or false'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'Aristotle says that a case should in justice be fought with the facts alone, but that because of the hearers we must attend to delivery, “not as being right, but necessary.” He does not make it the noblest part of the art or a substitute for argument.' } },
{ id:'how-learned', src:['ddc','cic_orat','cic_yonge','appown'], pids:['cic-arch-14','aug-ddc-3-4','aug-ddc-3-5','cic-deorat-1-146'],
  h:'<p><strong>How the art is learned</strong></p><p>Children learn to speak by hearing those who speak, long before they learn any rule of grammar, and an apprentice learns a craft by watching a master and working beside him. Rules come later, and they help most the person who already has some practice, because they let him see what he is doing and do it more surely.</p><p>The classical tradition held that rhetoric is learned in the same way. In Cicero’s dialogue <em>On the Orator</em>, Crassus says that “with regard to all precepts the case is this, not that orators by adhering to them have obtained distinction in eloquence, but that certain persons have noticed what men of eloquence practiced of their own accord, and formed rules accordingly; so that eloquence has not sprung from art, but art from eloquence” (<span class="lat">sic esse non eloquentiam ex artificio, sed artificium ex eloquentia natum</span>; <em>De oratore</em> I.146). He does not throw the precepts away, but he does not make them the source of eloquence. St. Augustine says the same: “men of quick intellect and glowing temperament find it easier to become eloquent by reading and listening to eloquent speakers than by following rules for eloquence” (<em>On Christian Doctrine</em> IV.3.4). And he draws the comparison with children: “as infants cannot learn to speak except by learning words and phrases from those who do speak, why should not men become eloquent without being taught any art of speech, simply by reading and learning the speeches of eloquent men, and by imitating them as far as they can?” (IV.3.5).</p><p>Imitation in this sense is not copying. Cicero, defending the poet Archias, praises the writers who have left “images of the bravest men, carefully elaborated,” “not merely for us to look at and gaze upon, but also for our imitation” (<em>For Archias</em> 14). We imitate a great speaker by learning to do what he does in a new case of our own.</p><p>For this reason the course gives only the core of the doctrine, enough to see in a real speech what the speaker is doing, and its exercises are built from real passages of orators, historians, and preachers, so that the doctrine is always learned from the speeches.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On Augustine’s account, how do most people become eloquent?',
      options:['By memorizing the rules of rhetoric before they read any author','By natural talent alone, without hearing or reading other speakers','By reading and hearing the eloquent, and imitating them','By studying grammar, which by itself makes a speaker eloquent'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Augustine says that people of quick intellect become eloquent more easily by reading and hearing eloquent speakers than by following rules, as infants learn to speak from those who speak. He does not say that talent alone suffices; he says that no one is eloquent without having read and heard the eloquent.' } }
]};

DECKS.orient = {
  title:'What Rhetoric Is', sub:'The means of persuasion and the kinds of speech',
  sources:['arist_rhet','cic_yonge','lysias_lamb','thuc_crawley','cic_cat','sallust_w','demosth'],
  panels:[
{ id:'three-pisteis', src:['arist_rhet','cic_yonge','lysias_lamb','thuc_crawley'], pids:['cic-milo-1','lys-12-18','thuc-arch-money'], taught:['pistis:cic-milo-1','pistis:lys-12-18','pistis:thuc-arch-money'],
  h:'<p><strong>The three means of persuasion</strong></p><p>When we take a doctor’s advice, we do so for several reasons at once. We trust him, because he seems to know his work and to be honest with us. He may also make us afraid of what will happen if we ignore him. And he gives reasons: the test shows this, and this treatment cures it. Some things that persuade us, however, are not the doctor’s work at all, such as the laboratory report or the law that requires a vaccination; he finds them ready to hand and only has to use them.</p><p>Aristotle draws the same distinctions. Some means of persuasion, or proofs (<span class="lat">pisteis</span>), are <strong>inartistic</strong> (<span class="lat">atechnoi</span>), because the speaker does not make them: “witnesses, evidence given under torture, written contracts, and so on.” Others are <strong>artistic</strong> (<span class="lat">entechnoi</span>), because the art itself supplies them, and of these there are three: “The first kind depends on the personal character of the speaker; the second on putting the audience into a certain frame of mind; the third on the proof, or apparent proof, provided by the words of the speech itself” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.2). The course uses the Greek names, as the tradition does:</p><p><strong>Ethos</strong> (character): the speech shows the speaker to be worthy of belief.<br><strong>Pathos</strong> (feeling): the speech puts the hearers into a certain frame of mind. As Aristotle says, “Our judgements when we are pleased and friendly are not the same as when we are pained and hostile.”<br><strong>Logos</strong> (speech, reasoning): the speech proves, or seems to prove, what it claims.</p><p>Each can be seen in real speeches. Cicero opens his defense of Milo by admitting that he is afraid, and that fear is unbecoming in one who defends “the bravest of men” (<em>Pro Milone</em> 1); the admission shows a modest speaker who honors his client, and that is ethos. The son in Lysias’s speech against Eratosthenes tells how the Thirty would not let his brother be buried from any of the family’s three houses, so that friends had to give a cloak or a pillow for the burial (Lysias 12.18); the story is meant to make the jurors indignant, and that is pathos. Archidamus argues that “war is a matter not so much of arms as of money, which makes arms of use” (Thucydides 1.83); that is a reason offered for judgment, and so it is logos.</p><p>Aristotle adds that ethos must come from the speech itself, not from what people thought of the speaker before he began, and that character “may almost be called the most effective means of persuasion he possesses.” Most passages, however, use more than one of the three at once.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A prosecutor reads aloud a letter that the defendant wrote years before the trial. On Aristotle’s division, what kind of proof is the letter?',
      options:['Pathos, because the hearers will be angry when they hear it','Inartistic, because the speaker uses it but does not make it','Logos, because a letter is an argument that the speech constructs','Ethos, because reading it shows the prosecutor to be honest'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Like the written contracts that Aristotle names, the letter is an inartistic proof: it was there before the speaker began, and he only has to use it. The artistic proofs (ethos, pathos, and logos) are those that the speech itself supplies.' } },
{ id:'chief-pistis', src:['arist_rhet','cic_cat','sallust_w','lysias_lamb'], pids:['cic-cat2-1','sal-caes-passion','lys-16-1'], taught:['pistis:cic-cat2-1','pistis:sal-caes-passion','pistis:lys-16-1'],
  h:'<p><strong>Which means is the speech chiefly using?</strong></p><p>We have seen that most passages use more than one means of persuasion. A sentence that gives a reason may also show the speaker to be fair and stir the hearers’ feelings. So the useful question is usually not which means is present, but which one the passage chiefly relies on, that is, what would be lost if it were taken away. Three tests help to answer it:</p><p><strong>Character</strong>: does the passage work by making us trust the speaker, through what he says of his own conduct, motives, or manner?<br><strong>Feeling</strong>: does it work by bringing the hearers into a state of mind, such as anger, fear, pity, or joy, in which they will judge the matter as the speaker wishes?<br><strong>The matter itself</strong>: does it work by giving a reason, a fact, or an example from which the conclusion follows?</p><p>Two confusions are common. The first is to take a speaker’s outburst for his own feeling only. When Cicero cries out to the people that Catiline “has gone, he has escaped us, evaded us, eluded us” (Second Catilinarian 1), the exultation is the speaker’s, but its work is to fill the hearers with relief and joy, so it is pathos; pathos is always a state of the hearer. The second is to take a passage about the passions for an appeal to them. When Caesar warns the senate that those who deliberate should “be influenced neither by hatred, affection, anger, nor pity” (Sallust, <em>Conspiracy of Catiline</em> 51), he names four passions, but he gives a reason for setting them aside, and that is logos.</p><p>Ethos, too, can be recognized by what the passage does. Mantitheus tells the Council that he would be grateful to his accusers, because those who are slandered gain most from being made to give an account of their lives (Lysias 16.1). He gives no argument about the charge yet; he shows himself to be a man with nothing to hide, and so the passage is chiefly ethos. A reputation announced from outside the speech, however, such as a letter of praise read out from someone else, is an inartistic proof and not ethos at all.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “I tremble as I say it: if we vote for this war, our sons will die on foreign shores.” What does the passage chiefly rely on?',
      options:['Ethos, because the speaker shows his own fear and so his honesty','Logos, because it states a consequence that follows from the vote','Pathos, because it puts the hearers in fear for their sons','An inartistic proof, because it reports a fact about the war'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'The speaker mentions his own trembling, but the passage works by making the hearers afraid for their sons; pathos is the state of the hearer, not the outburst of the speaker. A consequence is named, but no reason is given for it, so logos is not what the passage chiefly relies on.' } },
{ id:'three-species', src:['arist_rhet','demosth','lysias_lamb'], pids:['dem-phil1-31','lys-3-3','lys-2-80'], taught:['species:dem-phil1-31','species:lys-3-3','species:lys-2-80'],
  h:'<p><strong>The three species of speech</strong></p><p>Consider three occasions for a speech. At a town meeting, citizens argue whether to build a new road, and the question is whether it will do the town more good than harm. At a trial, the question is whether the accused did what he is charged with, and whether it was just. At a retirement dinner, no one is asked to decide anything; the speaker praises a colleague, and the guests judge how well the praise is given and how far it is deserved. The three differ in what the hearers are asked to judge, in the time the judgment concerns, and in the standard by which it is made.</p><p>Aristotle divides speeches into three kinds, or species, on exactly this ground: they are “determined by the three classes of listeners to speeches” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.3), since the hearer judges either the future, or the past, or the speech before him in the present:</p><p><strong>Deliberative</strong> (<span class="lat">symbouleutikon</span>): the assembly judges what is to be done; the time is the future, the end is the expedient or the harmful, and the speaker exhorts or dissuades.<br><strong>Forensic</strong> (<span class="lat">dikanikon</span>): the jury judges what has been done; the time is the past, the end is the just or the unjust, and the speaker accuses or defends.<br><strong>Epideictic</strong> (<span class="lat">epideiktikon</span>): the hearers judge the person praised and the speaker’s skill; the time is chiefly the present, the end is the noble or the shameful, and the speaker praises or blames.</p><p>(Rhys Roberts’s translation calls the first “political” and the third “the ceremonial oratory of display”; the course uses the usual names.)</p><p>Demosthenes tells the Athenians that “we must not carry on the war by means of isolated expeditions; we shall always be too late” (<em>First Philippic</em> 31–32); he advises a course for the future on the ground of advantage, so the speech is deliberative. The speaker of Lysias’s speech <em>Against Simon</em> says that he will now “relate to you the whole of the facts” of the case (3.3), because a jury must judge what was done; that is forensic. Lysias’s funeral speech says of the dead that by their nature “they are mourned as mortal,” but by their valor they “are lauded as immortal” (2.80); that is praise, and so epideictic.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'At a school’s founding day, the speaker praises the founder’s generosity and calls on no one to vote or to judge a deed. What is the species of the speech?',
      options:['Forensic, because it concerns what the founder did in the past','Deliberative, because the school will go on in the future','Epideictic, because it praises a person as noble','None of the three, because no decision is asked of the hearers'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Praise and blame of a person, judged by the noble and the shameful, make a speech epideictic. Its matter may come from the past, as Aristotle allows, but the hearers are not asked to judge the deed as just or unjust, or to decide what to do.' } },
{ id:'mixed', src:['arist_rhet','thuc_crawley','cic_cat','cic_yonge'], pids:['thuc-cleon-1','cic-cat1-13','cic-cat3-1','cic-arch-16'], taught:['species:thuc-cleon-1','species:cic-cat1-13','species:cic-cat3-1','species:cic-arch-16'],
  h:'<p><strong>Mixed speeches and the chief species</strong></p><p>Few real speeches keep to one species throughout. A lawyer defending a client may praise him at length; a speaker urging a new law may tell what happened when the old one failed. So a passage may look backward or praise someone and still belong to a deliberative speech, and we have to ask what the speech as a whole asks its hearers to decide now. That decision gives the chief species, and the rest of the speech serves it.</p><p>Aristotle allows for this. The epideictic speaker is concerned chiefly with the present, “though they often find it useful also to recall the past and to make guesses at the future” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.3), and he says that the other ends, such as justice or honor, are brought into a deliberative speech as subordinate to the expedient.</p><p>Four cases show how the test works. First, Cleon in the debate on Mytilene speaks of a revolt that is over, but he asks the assembly to keep its decree; since the vote is about what Athens will do, the speech is deliberative (Thucydides 3.37). Second, in the First Catilinarian Cicero charges Catiline with crimes already planned, but what he asks of the senate is action: “Let the disloyal then withdraw, let them separate themselves from the loyal” (1.32); the speech is therefore deliberative in its chief species, though forensic in much of its matter. Third, the Third Catilinarian reports to the people what has been discovered and gives thanks that the city has been “rescued from fire and sword” (3.1); no vote or verdict is asked, so the opening is epideictic. Fourth, the defense of Archias is forensic, because the jury must decide whether he is a citizen, yet much of it is praise of letters, which are “the food of youth, the delight of old age” (<em>For Archias</em> 16); the praise serves the verdict.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker before an assembly recalls at length how a neighboring city was destroyed after it trusted an ally, and ends by asking the assembly not to sign the treaty before it. What is the chief species?',
      options:['Forensic, because most of the speech tells of a past event','Epideictic, because the speech blames the ally for its conduct','Deliberative, because the assembly is asked what it should do now','Mixed, so that no chief species can be named'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'The chief species is named by what the hearers are asked to decide now. The story of the past serves as an example for a decision about the future, so the speech is deliberative.' } }
]};

DECKS.logos = {
  title:'Enthymeme, Example, and Signs', sub:'Invention · Logos',
  sources:['arist_rhet','cic_cat','cic_yonge','thuc_crawley','webster_w','antiphon'],
  panels:[
{ id:'enthymeme', src:['arist_rhet','cic_cat'], pids:['cic-cat1-2'], taught:['enth:e1'],
  h:'<p><strong>The enthymeme</strong></p><p>Someone at the door says, “Take an umbrella; the sky is black.” No one replies that the second statement does not prove the first, because we all supply what is left out: black skies usually bring rain. In ordinary speech we argue in this way all the time. We state a reason and a conclusion, and we leave out a general statement that the hearer already holds, because to say it aloud would be tedious or even insulting.</p><p>An argument of this kind is what the rhetoricians call an <strong>enthymeme</strong>. Logic studies the full argument, the syllogism, in which a conclusion follows from two premises (for example, “Every animal that breathes needs air; the whale breathes; therefore the whale needs air”). The enthymeme has the form of a syllogism, but it differs in two ways. First, it is about things that can be otherwise, such as what a man probably did or what a city should do, so its premises are usually likely rather than certain. Second, it leaves out what the hearers can supply. Aristotle says, “I call the enthymeme a rhetorical syllogism, and the example a rhetorical induction,” and he explains: “The enthymeme must consist of few propositions, fewer often than those which make up the normal syllogism. For if any of these propositions is a familiar fact, there is no need even to mention it; the hearer adds it himself” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.2). He also says that everyone who persuades by proof uses “either enthymemes or examples: there is no other way.” So the argument of a speech (its logos) takes one of these two forms.</p><p>Cicero’s First Catilinarian gives a clear case. “The senate is well aware of the facts, the consul can perceive them all; but the criminal still lives. Lives? Yes, lives; and even comes down to the senate” (1.2). What Cicero states is a fact that the senators can see for themselves. What turns the fact into a charge is a premise he does not state, because every senator already holds it: a man who plots the massacre of the senate ought not to sit in it. When we name that premise, we have understood the enthymeme. Naming it rightly, however, takes some care.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “He cannot be trusted with the city’s treasury; he cheated his own business partners.” What premise are the hearers meant to supply?',
      options:['The city’s treasury holds a great deal of public money','A man who cheats his partners is likely to cheat the city','He cannot be trusted with the city’s treasury at all','Partners who have been cheated ought to go to the courts'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The general statement that joins the reason (he cheated his partners) to the conclusion (he cannot be trusted with the treasury) is that a man who cheats his partners is likely to cheat the city. That the treasury holds much money is true but does not join the two, and the third option only repeats the conclusion.' } },
{ id:'find-premise', src:['arist_rhet','thuc_crawley'], pids:['thuc-per-141'],
  h:'<p><strong>How to find the missing premise</strong></p><p>We have seen that the enthymeme leaves out a premise that the hearers hold. To find it, we can take three steps:</p><p><strong>The conclusion</strong>: what does the speaker want the hearers to believe or to do?<br><strong>The stated reason</strong>: what does he offer in support of it?<br><strong>The joining premise</strong>: what general statement, already held by the hearers, would make the conclusion follow from the reason?</p><p>To take a simple example, a parent says, “You may not go out tonight; you have not finished your homework.” The conclusion is that the child may not go out; the reason is that the homework is not finished; and the joining premise is that homework must be finished before going out. The test of the premise is that, once it is added, the argument is complete: the conclusion follows from the reason and the premise together.</p><p>The tempting wrong answers usually fail in one of three ways. Some are true, or plausible, but do not join the reason to the conclusion (“Homework is good for children”). Some only repeat the conclusion in other words (“Children must stay home on school nights”). And some state the contrary of what the speaker assumes, so that, if the hearers held it, they would reject the argument (“Children may go out whether or not their work is done”).</p><p>Sometimes the speaker leaves out the conclusion instead, because the hearers can draw it themselves. Pericles tells the Athenians that “Capital, it must be remembered, maintains a war more than forced contributions. Farmers are a class of men that are always more ready to serve in person than in purse” (Thucydides 1.141). He states both premises, and he lets the assembly draw the conclusion: the Peloponnesians, a people of farmers without capital, cannot keep up a long war. In such a case the first step, naming the conclusion, is the one that needs the most care.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “We should not elect him captain; he has never sailed in a storm.” Which option only repeats the conclusion instead of joining the reason to it?',
      options:['A captain should be one who has already sailed through storms','Storms at sea are more frequent in winter than in summer','He is not the man whom we should choose to be our captain','Any sailor at all can command a ship whatever his experience'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'“He is not the man whom we should choose” says again what the speaker concludes. The joining premise is that a captain should be one who has sailed through storms; the remark about winter is true but joins nothing, and the last option is the contrary of what the speaker assumes.' } },
{ id:'maxims', src:['arist_rhet','cic_yonge','eng_cer'], pids:['cic-milo-2','eng-jc-antony-evil-lives'], taught:['fig:cic-milo-2:sententia','fig:eng-jc-antony-evil-lives:sententia'],
  h:'<p><strong>Maxims</strong></p><p>Many of the premises that hearers supply are proverbs and common sayings: “Haste makes waste,” “A stitch in time saves nine,” “Honesty is the best policy.” When someone says, “Let us not decide tonight,” the hearers may supply “Haste makes waste” without hearing it, and when the saying is spoken, it can stand by itself as the premise of the argument.</p><p>Aristotle calls such a saying a <strong>maxim</strong> (<span class="lat">gnome</span>). It is a general statement, not a particular fact, and it is not about any subject whatever “but only about questions of practical conduct, courses of conduct to be chosen or avoided” (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.21). Because the enthymeme reasons about such conduct, he says, “It is therefore roughly true that the premisses or conclusions of Enthymemes, considered apart from the rest of the argument, are Maxims.” A maxim with its reason added is an enthymeme; a maxim said alone is the premise or the conclusion of one.</p><p>Cicero, defending Milo for the killing of Clodius, says, “When arms speak, the laws are silent” (<em>Pro Milone</em> 11). The maxim is general and concerns conduct, and in the speech it serves as a premise: if the laws are silent when a man is attacked with weapons, then a man who defends himself against armed attack has not broken them. Shakespeare’s Antony uses a maxim in the same way at Caesar’s funeral: “The evil that men do lives after them, The good is oft interred with their bones” (<em>Julius Caesar</em> III.2). The hearers apply it to Caesar at once, and so they begin to ask whether the evil they have heard of him is the whole truth.</p><p>When such a general statement is heard as a striking form of words, the tradition also calls it a figure, the <span class="lat">sententia</span>; the same saying can be considered as a premise of the argument and as a figure of style.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Which of these is a maxim in Aristotle’s sense?',
      options:['Caesar crossed the Rubicon with a single legion','The sum of the angles of a triangle is two right angles','He who hesitates when the city is in danger betrays it','Our ambassadors returned from Sparta on the third day'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'A maxim is a general statement about conduct to be chosen or avoided. The statements about Caesar and the ambassadors are particular facts, and the statement about the triangle is general but is not about conduct.' } },
{ id:'example', src:['arist_rhet','thuc_crawley'], pids:['thuc-herm-examples'],
  h:'<p><strong>Example</strong></p><p>Someone argues, “The last time we skipped the inspection, the roof leaked, so we should not skip it this year.” Another says, “Choosing the treasurer by lot is like choosing a ship’s pilot by lot.” Both arguments persuade by setting a case beside the one in question, and the hearers are asked to judge the new case as they judge the old one.</p><p>This is the <strong>example</strong> (<span class="lat">paradeigma</span>), which Aristotle calls a rhetorical induction, that is, an argument from particular cases to what holds in a like case. He divides it into two kinds: “one consisting in the mention of actual past facts, the other in the invention of facts by the speaker” (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.20). The invented example is either an illustrative parallel or a fable. His instance of the historical example is a speaker who urges war against the king of Persia, because Darius and Xerxes each crossed into Greece once they had seized Egypt; therefore the present king, if he seizes Egypt, will cross as well. His instance of the parallel is Socrates’ argument that “Public officials ought not to be selected by lot. That is like using the lot to select athletes, instead of choosing those who are fit for the contest.”</p><p>Hermocrates, urging the cities of Sicily to make peace, gives many examples at once: “many before now have tried to chastise a wrongdoer, and failing to punish their enemy have not even saved themselves; while many who have trusted in force to gain an advantage, instead of gaining anything more, have been doomed to lose what they had” (Thucydides 4.62). He does not name the cities, because his hearers know such cases from their own history; the force of the argument lies in how many and how like they are. An example persuades only so far as the cases are really alike, so the reply to an example is usually to show a difference between the cases.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker urges the council to repair the dam, because a neighboring town that neglected its dam was flooded last spring. What kind of proof is this?',
      options:['A historical example, drawn from a real past case','A fable, which the speaker has invented to make his point','A maxim, which states a general rule about conduct','A necessary sign, from which the conclusion cannot fail'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'The flood in the neighboring town actually happened, and it is set beside the present case so that the council will judge this case in the same way; that is a historical example. It is not invented, it is not a general statement, and it does not prove the conclusion necessarily.' } },
{ id:'signs', src:['arist_rhet','webster_w','antiphon'], pids:['eng-webster-knapp-there','ant-3.2.4'],
  h:'<p><strong>Signs and likelihoods</strong></p><p>Where there is smoke, there is fire, and a woman who is nursing an infant has recently given birth: from what we see we infer what we do not see. But a man seen running from a house on the night of a theft may be the thief, or he may be late for a train. Some signs, then, prove their conclusion, and others only make it likely, and a speaker must know which kind he is using.</p><p>Aristotle says that enthymemes are drawn from likelihoods and signs. A <strong>likelihood</strong> (<span class="lat">eikos</span>) is “a thing that usually happens” in matters that can turn out otherwise, as that a man who hates another is more likely to harm him. A <strong>sign</strong> is a fact from which another fact is inferred. The infallible sign is called a “complete proof” (<span class="lat">tekmerion</span>): “The fact that he has a fever is a sign that he is ill,” or “The fact that she is giving milk is a sign that she has lately borne a child.” The fallible sign has no special name, and Aristotle’s example is “The fact that he breathes fast is a sign that he has a fever,” which can be refuted “since a man may breathe hard without having a fever” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.2). To present a fallible sign as if it were a complete proof is a fault in argument, and an opponent can expose it.</p><p>Daniel Webster, prosecuting a man for a murder in Salem, piles up signs of presence: “He was there before the murder; he was there after the murder; he was there clandestinely, unwilling to be seen” (Argument in the Knapp trial, 1830). None of these proves that the man was present at the murder itself, but together they make it likely, and Webster asks the jury to judge by their number. In Antiphon’s javelin case, by contrast, the signs are agreed by both sides (the boy is dead, and the javelin struck him), and the defense argues that they do not settle the question: because the boy ran under the javelin’s path, it was the boy, not the thrower, who missed (Second Tetralogy 3.2.4). When the signs are granted, the dispute moves to what they mean, and that is a question of the issue on which the case stands.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A prosecutor says that the accused must have been at the scene, because mud like the mud of the riverbank was found on his boots. What kind of sign is this?',
      options:['A complete proof (tekmerion), because the mud cannot be explained otherwise','A maxim, because it is a general statement about conduct','An example, because it sets one case beside another like it','A fallible sign, because the mud could have come from elsewhere'],
      correct:3,
      explain:'Mud like that of the riverbank makes his presence likely, but the same mud could have been picked up elsewhere, so the sign is fallible. Aristotle reserves the name tekmerion for a sign from which the conclusion cannot fail, as illness follows from fever.' } }
]};

DECKS.issue = {
  title:'The Issue and the Topics', sub:'Invention · Where the case stands, and where arguments are found',
  sources:['cic_inv','cic_part','cic_topica','arist_freese','arist_rhet','cic_yonge','lysias_lamb','thuc_crawley','demosth','plato_jowett'],
  panels:[
{ id:'four-issues', src:['cic_inv','cic_part','lysias_lamb','plato_jowett'], pids:['lys-1-1'],
  h:'<p><strong>Where the case stands: the four issues</strong></p><p>A child is accused of taking a cookie from the jar. He can answer in four quite different ways. He can say, “I didn’t take it.” He can say, “I took it, but I only borrowed it; I was going to put it back.” He can say, “I took it, but Mother said I could.” Or he can say, “You can’t punish me for it; you aren’t my mother.” Each answer puts the dispute in a different place, and each needs different arguments. Before a speaker looks for arguments, then, he has to know where the case stands, that is, what exactly is in dispute.</p><p>The rhetoricians call this point the <strong>issue</strong> (Greek <span class="lat">stasis</span>, Latin <span class="lat">status</span> or <span class="lat">constitutio</span>). Cicero says that it arises from the meeting of charge and answer: “A stating of a case is the first conflict of causes arising from a repulse of an accusation; in this way. ‘You did so and so;’—‘I did not do so;’—or, ‘it was lawful for me to do so’” (<em>De inventione</em> I.8). He names four issues:</p><p><strong>Fact</strong> (the conjectural issue): whether the thing was done, or is so, or will be.<br><strong>Definition</strong>: the act is granted, and the dispute is about the name it should bear.<br><strong>Quality</strong>: the act and its name are granted, and the dispute is about its character, whether it was just or unjust, right or wrong.<br><strong>Transference</strong> (Yonge’s “statement by way of demurrer”): the dispute is whether the action is rightly brought, that is, by the right person, against the right person, before the right court, at the right time, under the right law, and for the right penalty.</p><p>The child’s four answers are in this order. Real cases show the same issues. Socrates denies that he corrupted the young, so his defense stands chiefly on fact (Plato, <em>Apology</em>). Euphiletus, in Lysias’s first speech, admits that he killed Eratosthenes, whom he caught with his wife, and argues that the law allowed it; he opens by asking the jurors to judge as if the wrong had been done to them, since “not one of you but would be indignant at what has been done” (Lysias 1.1). His case therefore stands on quality.</p><p>Not every author counts four. The <em>Rhetorica ad Herennium</em> counts three issues (I.11.18), and in the <em>Topics</em> and the <em>Partitions</em> Cicero names three himself: fact, definition, and quality. The course follows the four of <em>De inventione</em>, and the issue of quality has divisions of its own.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A man accused of trespass says, “I crossed the field, but it is a public road, not private land.” On which issue does his defense stand?',
      options:['Fact, because he denies that he crossed the field at all','Definition, because he grants the act and disputes its name','Quality, because he says that crossing was right in the circumstances','Transference, because he says the wrong court is trying him'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'He grants the act (he crossed the field) and disputes whether it can be called trespass, since the land is a road; that is the issue of definition. He does not deny the act, defend it as right, or object to the court.' } },
{ id:'quality', src:['cic_inv','cic_yonge'], pids:['cic-milo-10','cic-lig-30'], taught:['stasis:st-ligarius'],
  h:'<p><strong>The divisions of the issue of quality</strong></p><p>We have seen that a case stands on the issue of quality when the act and its name are granted and the dispute is about whether it was right. But there are several ways of defending an act that is admitted. A man who broke a window may say that breaking it was right, or that he broke it to save a child from a fire, or that the owner had broken his first, or that someone pushed him, or simply that he is sorry and asks to be forgiven.</p><p>Cicero divides the issue of quality in the same way (<em>De inventione</em> II.23–36). In the <strong>absolute</strong> division the act is defended as right in itself. In the others, which he calls assumptive, the defense brings in something from outside the act:</p><p><strong>Comparison</strong>: the act was done for the sake of a greater good (the window was broken to save the child).<br><strong>Retort</strong> (the retorting of the accusation): the act answered a wrong that the other party did first.<br><strong>Removal</strong>: the blame is laid on another person or thing (Yonge calls it the transference of an accusation, but it is not the fourth issue).<br><strong>Concession</strong>: the accused does not defend the act as right, but he excuses it, either by <strong>ignorance</strong> (he did not know), by <strong>accident</strong> (fortune prevented what he intended), or by <strong>necessity</strong> (he was compelled by force), or else he asks only for pardon, which is called <strong>deprecation</strong>.</p><p>Questions help to tell them apart. Is the act defended as right in itself (absolute), or because of a greater good (comparison)? Does the defense blame the other party for a wrong done first (retort), or a third party or thing (removal)? Does it excuse the act without defending it (ignorance, accident, necessity), or neither defend nor excuse it but ask for mercy (deprecation)?</p><p>Cicero’s defense of Milo asks, “But what death can be unjust when inflicted on a secret plotter and robber?” (<em>Pro Milone</em> 10); the slain man is said to have attacked first, so this is retort. In his speech for Ligarius, Cicero tells Caesar that he will not plead as men plead before a judge, but as before a parent: “I have erred; I have acted rashly; I repent” (<em>For Ligarius</em> 30). That is deprecation.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A ship’s captain who threw the cargo overboard in a storm is sued by its owner. He says, “I did it to save the ship and every life aboard.” Which division of quality does he use?',
      options:['Retort, because the owner wronged him before the storm','Deprecation, because he asks only for pardon','Comparison, because he acted for a greater good','Removal, because he lays the blame on another person'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'He admits the act and defends it by the greater good it served, the safety of the ship and the crew; that is comparison. He does not blame the owner or anyone else, and he does not merely ask for pardon.' } },
{ id:'what-topic', src:['cic_topica','arist_freese','thuc_crawley','demosth'],
  h:'<p><strong>What a topic is</strong></p><p>Someone who has to argue a question and has nothing yet to say does not wait for inspiration. He asks a set of familiar questions. What is the thing? What caused it? What will follow from it? What is it like, and what is it unlike? What do those who know say about it? Each question leads to a place in which an argument may be found, and the same questions serve whether the subject is a law, a war, or a marriage.</p><p>The classical tradition calls such a place a <strong>topic</strong> (Greek <span class="lat">topos</span>, Latin <span class="lat">locus</span>, both meaning “place”). Cicero defines it this way: “that a topic is the seat of an argument, and that an argument is a reason which causes men to believe a thing which would otherwise be doubtful” (<em>Topica</em> 8). Aristotle calls the topic “a head under which several enthymemes are included” (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.26.1). A topic, then, is not itself an argument but a general line of reasoning, such as the more and less, definition, or cause, from which arguments on any subject can be drawn. That is why one topic can supply arguments in very different cases.</p><p>Aristotle gives a list of twenty-eight topics in <em>Rhetoric</em> II.23, with examples from orators and poets; the course takes twenty-four of them. Cicero gives a shorter list in his <em>Topics</em>, which he wrote for the lawyer Trebatius, with examples from Roman civil law. The two lists overlap but are not the same, and they sometimes give the same name to different things (both have “definition” and the “parts,” for example, but Aristotle has topics such as turning the charge that Cicero’s list does not have). So the two lists are best learned separately, each in its own terms, and the exercise on the topics keeps each question to one list. We take Aristotle’s list first, in two groups.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On Cicero’s account, what is a topic?',
      options:['A finished argument that can be used in any case without change','The seat of an argument, from which arguments are drawn','The subject of the speech, such as a war or a law','A figure of speech that makes an argument memorable'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Cicero calls a topic “the seat of an argument.” It is a place where arguments are found, not a finished argument and not the subject of the speech, and it belongs to invention, not to style.' } },
{ id:'arist-topics-1', src:['arist_freese','arist_rhet','thuc_crawley','appown'], pids:['thuc-per-4','thuc-fun-2'],
  h:'<p><strong>Aristotle’s topics drawn from words, kinds, and comparisons</strong></p><p>Many of Aristotle’s topics work through the meaning of words, the parts and kinds of things, and the comparison of one case with another. Here are twelve of them, each with the name the course uses and a simple example of our own:</p><p><strong>Opposites</strong>: what holds for a thing is examined in its opposite. “If laziness ruins a farm, diligence will save it.”<br><strong>Inflections</strong>: what holds of a word holds of the forms derived from it. “If it is shameful to act like a coward, cowardice is shameful.”<br><strong>Relative terms</strong>: what is true of one party holds for the other. “If the teacher rightly gave the mark, the student rightly received it.”<br><strong>More and less</strong>: if the likelier case fails, the less likely must fail too, and if the less likely holds, the likelier holds. “He cheated his own brother, so he will surely cheat a stranger.”<br><strong>Time</strong>: what would have been granted earlier is due now as well. “If you would have rewarded me for promising this service, reward me now that I have done it.”<br><strong>Definition</strong>: the conclusion is drawn from what the thing is. “Courage is enduring danger for a good end; so the soldier who held the bridge was brave.”<br><strong>Meaning of a name</strong>: a name is taken to show what the person or thing is. “He is rightly called Constant, for he has kept faith with us for twenty years.”<br><strong>Division</strong>: the possible cases are listed and each is set aside. “He took the money to keep it, to give it away, or to pay a debt; but he kept none, gave none away, and owed nothing.”<br><strong>Parts</strong>: the whole is examined by going through each of its parts. “Is he a good soldier? Consider his courage, his discipline, and his skill.”<br><strong>Induction</strong>: a general rule is drawn from several similar cases. “Every captain who trusted that pilot ran aground; so he should not be trusted.”<br><strong>Analogy</strong>: what is granted in one case holds in its counterpart. “If parents may keep a child from a dangerous friend, a city may keep its young from a dangerous teacher.”<br><strong>Previous judgment</strong>: those whose judgment must be respected have already decided the question. “The court acquitted another man on this very evidence.”</p><p>Pericles uses more and less in the funeral speech: “if our more remote ancestors deserve praise, much more do our own fathers” (Thucydides 2.36). And he argues from the meaning of a name when he says of the Athenian constitution that “Its administration favours the many instead of the few; this is why it is called a democracy” (2.37). The other twelve topics concern acts, motives, and persons.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker argues, “If the founders of this school deserve our thanks, much more do the teachers who have kept it open through the war.” From which topic is the argument drawn?',
      options:['Previous judgment','Division','Relative terms','More and less'],
      correct:3,
      explain:'The argument says that if the lesser claim (the founders’) deserves thanks, the greater claim (the teachers’) deserves it much more; that is the topic of more and less. It does not appeal to a decision already made, list possibilities, or reason from one party to its correlative.' } },
{ id:'arist-topics-2', src:['arist_freese','arist_rhet','thuc_crawley','demosth','appown'], pids:['dem-phil3-8','thuc-per-143'],
  h:'<p><strong>Aristotle’s topics drawn from acts, motives, and persons</strong></p><p>We have seen that some of Aristotle’s topics work through words and comparisons. The rest consider what people do and why they do it, and they are used most often in the courts, where the question is what a man did and whether it was right. Here are the other twelve, again with examples of our own:</p><p><strong>Turning the charge</strong>: the accusation is turned against the accuser. “You accuse me of taking bribes, but you were convicted of it yourself.”<br><strong>Consequence</strong>: a thing is urged or opposed for what follows from it. “A new road will bring trade as well as noise; since the trade matters more, let us build it.”<br><strong>Two opposites</strong>: good and evil follow on each of two contrary courses, so that each can be argued for and against. “If he goes into business, he will grow rich but have no leisure; if he stays at the university, he will have leisure but stay poor.”<br><strong>Same result</strong>: things that have the same result are held to be alike. “To refuse to vote is the same as to vote for the tyrant, since either way he rules.”<br><strong>Change of choice</strong>: people now choose the contrary of what they chose before. “When we were poor we gave to the hospital; now that we are rich, shall we refuse it?”<br><strong>Possible motive</strong>: a motive that a person could have had is taken as the one he had. “He wanted his partner’s share of the business, and so he must have planned the fraud.”<br><strong>The incredible</strong>: a thing so unlikely would not be said unless it were true. “No one would invent so strange a story; it must have happened.”<br><strong>Contradiction</strong>: the opponent’s words and deeds, or his statements at different times, are at variance. “He says that he loves the city, but he has never once served it.”<br><strong>Cause of a slander</strong>: the reason for a false opinion is given, so that the opinion falls. “She was seen leaving his house at night not as his mistress but as the nurse of his dying mother.”<br><strong>Cause</strong>: the effect is judged by whether its cause was present. “He could not have set the fire, because he had neither fuel nor flame.”<br><strong>Contrary acts</strong>: the two contrary courses are examined together, and it is asked whether a better course was open. “If he had meant to cheat us, he would have sold the land at once, not held it for ten years.”<br><strong>Mistakes</strong>: an error made before is used to accuse or to defend. “You trusted such an ally once and were betrayed; do not trust this one.”</p><p>Demosthenes uses the topic of contradiction when he asks what the Athenians can do if Philip “holds out to you the name of peace, while his own acts are acts of war” (<em>Third Philippic</em> 8). Pericles argues from consequence when he warns that a single victory at sea would only bring another battle, whereas “a reverse involves the loss of our allies, the source of our strength” (Thucydides 1.143).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'An accused man says, “My accuser charges me with cowardice, but he was the first to run from the battle.” From which topic is the argument drawn?',
      options:['The incredible','Turning the charge','Cause of a slander','Mistakes'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'He turns the accusation of cowardice upon the accuser, which is the topic of turning the charge. He does not argue that the story is too strange to be false, explain why a false opinion arose, or appeal to an earlier error.' } },
{ id:'cic-topics', src:['cic_topica','cic_yonge','appown'], pids:['cic-phil2-113','cic-man-28','cic-arch-8'],
  h:'<p><strong>Cicero’s topics</strong></p><p>We have seen that Cicero’s list is shorter than Aristotle’s and drawn from the law. He divides it by where the argument comes from: from the subject itself, from things related to it, or from outside it (<em>Topica</em> 8–24). The names below are the ones the course uses, each with a simple example of our own.</p><p>From the subject itself:</p><p><strong>Definition</strong>: the matter is unfolded by saying what it is. “A will states what a man wishes done with his property after his death; this paper says nothing of his property, so it is not a will.”<br><strong>Enumeration of the parts</strong>: the whole is examined part by part. “A man becomes a citizen by birth, by grant, or by enrollment; he has none of these, so he is not a citizen.”<br><strong>Notation</strong>: the argument rests on what a word originally meant. “The senate takes its name from <span class="lat">senex</span>, an old man, so it should be a council of the experienced.”</p><p>From things related to the subject:</p><p><strong>Conjugates</strong>: words of the same root. “If the act was just, the one who did it acted justly.”<br><strong>Genus</strong>: what holds of the kind holds of what is under it. “Animals are not allowed in the building; a cat is an animal.”<br><strong>Species</strong>: which form of the kind is meant. “The will leaves him ‘the wine’; was the old wine in the cellar meant, or only this year’s?”<br><strong>Similarity</strong>: like cases are judged alike. “If a tenant must repair what he damages, so must the man who borrows a car.”<br><strong>Difference</strong>: things that seem alike are shown to be unlike. “A gift is not a loan, so it need not be returned.”<br><strong>Contraries</strong>: what is true of a thing is denied of its contrary. “If cruelty is hateful, mercy is to be loved.”<br><strong>Adjuncts</strong>: what accompanied the act, such as the place, the hour, and the means. “He was near the house at that hour, with a knife.”<br><strong>Antecedents</strong>: what went before. “They quarreled the day before.”<br><strong>Consequents</strong>: what followed. “He left the city the next morning.”<br><strong>Contradictories</strong>: two claims that cannot stand together. “He cannot have been both in Rome and in Capua on that day.”<br><strong>Efficient causes</strong>: what produced the effect. “The river undermined the piers, so the builder is not to blame for the fall of the bridge.”<br><strong>Effects</strong>: what has been brought about. “The policy has emptied the treasury, so it was a bad policy.”<br><strong>Comparison</strong>: a case is set beside one greater, equal, or less. “If the law protects a man’s cattle, much more does it protect his children.”</p><p>From outside the subject:</p><p><strong>Authority</strong>: a weighty opinion or testimony is brought from outside the case. Cicero says that such arguments “are deduced chiefly from authority,” and that the Greeks call them inartistic, “that is, devoid of art” (<em>Topica</em> 24).</p><p>Cicero’s speeches show the same topics at work. He argues from definition when he says, “Peace is liberty in tranquillity; slavery is the worst of all evils” (<em>Philippics</em> 2.113), and from comparison when he asks, “Who, then, ever was, or ought to have been, better acquainted with military affairs than this man?” (<em>On the Manilian Law</em> 28). For Archias he brings authority from outside the case, Lucullus, “who will tell you not that he thinks it, but that he knows it” (<em>For Archias</em> 8).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A lawyer argues, “The defendant bought poison two days before his uncle died.” From which of Cicero’s topics is the argument drawn?',
      options:['Consequents','Antecedents','Contradictories','Notation'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The purchase of poison went before the death, so the argument rests on antecedents. Consequents are what followed the act, contradictories are claims that cannot stand together, and notation rests on what a word originally meant.' } }
]};

DECKS.ethos = {
  title:'The Speaker and the Hearers', sub:'Invention · Ethos and Pathos',
  sources:['arist_rhet','aquinas_st','greg','thuc_crawley','lysias_lamb','cic_yonge','cic_cat','plato_jowett'],
  panels:[
{ id:'trust', src:['arist_rhet','thuc_crawley'], pids:['thuc-per-60'], taught:['ethos:thuc-per-60'],
  h:'<p><strong>Why we trust a speaker</strong></p><p>When we choose a mechanic, we want three things. He must know the work; he must be honest, so that he does not charge for repairs he did not make; and he must want our good, so that he does not sell us what we do not need. A mechanic who lacks any one of the three is not to be trusted, however good the other two may be.</p><p>Aristotle gives the same three reasons for trusting a speaker. There are three things that inspire confidence in the speaker’s character, “the three, namely, that induce us to believe a thing apart from any proof of it: good sense, good moral character, and goodwill” (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.1). The tradition uses the Greek names:</p><p><strong>Practical wisdom</strong> (<span class="lat">phronesis</span>): the speaker knows what should be done.<br><strong>Virtue</strong> (<span class="lat">arete</span>): he is honest, and he will say what he thinks.<br><strong>Goodwill</strong> (<span class="lat">eunoia</span>): he wishes the good of his hearers.</p><p>He also gives the reason why all three are needed: men give false advice either because they lack good sense, or because, judging rightly, they do not say what they think out of bad character, or because, being “both sensible and upright, but not well disposed to their hearers,” they “may fail in consequence to recommend what they know to be the best course.”</p><p>Pericles, facing an assembly angry with him for the war, defends himself in exactly these terms. He says that he is “second to no man either in knowledge of the proper policy, or in the ability to expound it,” and that he is “not only a patriot but an honest one,” and then he shows why each is needed: knowledge without the power to explain it is useless, a man without love for his country “would be but a cold advocate for her interests,” and if his patriotism were not proof against bribery, “everything would go for a price” (Thucydides 2.60). As we have seen, this trust must be won by the speech itself, so we must ask how a speech shows character.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'An adviser to a town council knows exactly what the town needs and wishes it well, but he is known to change his advice for a bribe. On Aristotle’s account, which of the three does he lack?',
      options:['Practical wisdom (phronesis)','Goodwill (eunoia)','Virtue (arete)','None of the three'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'He has the knowledge (practical wisdom) and the wish for the town’s good (goodwill), but a man who changes his advice for a bribe does not say what he thinks; he lacks virtue. Aristotle says that a man who lacks any one of the three may give false advice.' } },
{ id:'shows-character', src:['arist_rhet','lysias_lamb','cic_yonge','thuc_crawley'], pids:['lys-24-1','cic-man-1','thuc-alc-1'], taught:['pistis:lys-24-1','pistis:cic-man-1'],
  h:'<p><strong>How a speech shows character</strong></p><p>We judge people by what they choose, not only by what they say about themselves. A man who tells us that he is modest is not believed for that reason, whereas a man who admits a fault that he could have hidden is believed at once. In the same way a speech shows the speaker’s character through the choices it makes: what he admits and what he refuses, how he speaks of his opponents, and what he says about why he is speaking at all.</p><p>Several choices of this kind recur in the orators. First, the speaker declines to flatter, or admits the limits of his ability. Second, he explains that he speaks unwillingly, forced into court by an opponent, so that he appears as a quiet citizen and not a lover of lawsuits (the Greek name for such a man is <span class="lat">apragmon</span>, one who minds his own business). Third, he welcomes the inquiry instead of fearing it. The invalid in Lysias begins, “I can almost find it in me to be grateful to my accuser, gentlemen of the Council, for having involved me in these proceedings,” because now he has a reason to give an account of his life (Lysias 24.1); the gratitude shows a man with nothing to hide. Cicero, giving his first speech from the speakers’ platform, says that he had not dared, “on account of my youth, to intrude upon the authority of this place” (<em>On the Manilian Law</em> 1), and the modesty disposes the people to trust him when he does speak.</p><p>The opposite choice shows character too. Alcibiades opens by saying, “I have a better right to command than others,” and goes on to boast of the seven chariots he entered at Olympia (Thucydides 6.16). His argument is that his splendor brought the city fame, but the boast also shows the hearers a man who seeks his own glory, and so it gives his opponents a reason to distrust his advice.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A defendant begins, “I would rather have kept silent about my neighbor’s wrongs, and I come to court only because he has left me no other way.” What does the opening chiefly show?',
      options:['The practical wisdom of a man who knows the law well','The character of a quiet citizen forced into court','The passion of anger, aroused in the jurors against the neighbor','An inartistic proof drawn from the record of the trial'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'By saying that he speaks only because he must, the speaker shows himself to be a quiet man who does not seek lawsuits, which is a showing of character in the speech. He says nothing yet about the law, and the opening does not chiefly stir the jurors’ anger.' } },
{ id:'passions', src:['arist_rhet','aquinas_st','appown'],
  h:'<p><strong>The passions, and how a speaker reaches them</strong></p><p>The same news angers one man and frightens another, and it leaves a third unmoved. A report that a rival city is building ships may anger those who think it an insult, frighten those who live on the coast, and mean nothing to those who never go near the sea. So a speaker who wants to move his hearers must know not only what each feeling is, but also toward whom it is felt and for what reasons.</p><p>Aristotle treats the passions (Greek <span class="lat">pathe</span>, the feelings that change our judgments) in the second book of the <em>Rhetoric</em>, and for each he asks three questions: “(1) what the state of mind of angry people is, (2) who the people are with whom they usually get angry, and (3) on what grounds they get angry with them” (II.1). “It is not enough to know one or even two of these points,” he says. He treats anger and calmness, friendship and enmity (love and hatred), fear and confidence, shame, kindness, pity, indignation, envy, and emulation (II.2–11). The course takes ten of them: anger, love, hatred, kindness, fear, confidence, shame, pity, indignation, and emulation.</p><p>St. Thomas Aquinas maps the passions in another way, by their object (<em>Summa theologiae</em> I–II, q. 23). The <strong>concupiscible</strong> passions concern good or evil “absolutely,” that is, simply as pleasant or painful; the <strong>irascible</strong> passions concern good or evil as “arduous, through being difficult to obtain or avoid” (a. 1). He counts eleven: “in the concupiscible faculty there are three couples of passions; viz. love and hatred, desire and aversion, joy and sadness. In like manner there are three groups in the irascible faculty; viz. hope and despair, fear and daring, and anger which has not contrary passion” (a. 4).</p><p>Thomas’s eleven do not name pity, shame, kindness, indignation, or emulation, and he calls the contrary of fear “daring” (<span class="lat">audacia</span>), where the course, following Aristotle, says “confidence.” Shame, however, Thomas places himself. Treating the kinds of fear, he says that one evil a man fears is “the disgrace which damages him in the opinion of others. And thus, if disgrace is feared in a deed that is yet to be done, there is ‘shamefacedness’; if, however, it be a deed already done, there is ‘shame’” (I–II, q. 41, a. 4). Shame is therefore a kind of fear, and since fear is irascible, so is shame. For the other four the course places Aristotle’s passions on Thomas’s division by Thomas’s own criterion, and this placement is the course’s, not Thomas’s:</p><p><strong>Concupiscible</strong>: love and hatred (as in Thomas); pity, which is a sorrow at another’s evil; and kindness, which is a love that wishes another’s good.<br><strong>Irascible</strong>: anger and fear (as in Thomas); shame, which Thomas counts as a kind of fear; confidence, which is Thomas’s daring; indignation, which, like anger, rises against an evil to be resisted; and emulation, which strives toward a good that is hard to attain.</p><p>We take the ten passions in three groups.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On St. Thomas’s division, why is fear an irascible passion?',
      options:['Because it concerns an evil that is arduous, that is, hard to avoid','Because every passion that is painful belongs to the irascible faculty','Because it is a kind of anger directed at a future danger','Because it concerns good and evil simply as pleasant or painful'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'The irascible passions concern good or evil as arduous, difficult to obtain or avoid; fear regards an evil that is hard to avoid. Not every painful passion is irascible (sadness is concupiscible), and good and evil taken simply as pleasant or painful are the object of the concupiscible passions.' } },
{ id:'anger-love', src:['arist_rhet','cic_cat','cic_yonge','thuc_crawley'], pids:['cic-cat4-s12','cic-arch-18','thuc-per-5'],
  h:'<p><strong>Anger, love, hatred, and kindness</strong></p><p>A man insulted in public by a neighbor is angry with that neighbor; he wants him to feel it and to be sorry. A man who hears of thieves in general hates thieves, though no thief has wronged him. A mother loves her son and wants his good for his own sake, and a stranger who carries an old woman’s bags up the stairs, expecting nothing, shows kindness. These four feelings are near one another, but they differ in their object and in what they seek.</p><p>Aristotle defines them as follows (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.2, II.4, II.7):</p><p><strong>Anger</strong>: “an impulse, accompanied by pain, to a conspicuous revenge for a conspicuous slight directed without justification towards what concerns oneself or towards what concerns one’s friends.” It is therefore felt toward “some particular individual” and not toward man in general.<br><strong>Love</strong> (friendly feeling): “wishing for him what you believe to be good things, not for your own sake but for his, and being inclined, so far as you can, to bring these things about.”<br><strong>Hatred</strong> (enmity): unlike anger, it “is directed also against classes: we all hate any thief and any informer”; anger “aims at giving pain to its object,” hatred “at doing him harm,” and “anger can be cured by time; but hatred cannot.”<br><strong>Kindness</strong>: “helpfulness towards some one in need, not in return for anything, nor for the advantage of the helper himself, but for that of the person helped.”</p><p>The orators reach each of them. Cicero asks the senate whether a father who finds his children killed by a slave and does not take “the bitterest vengeance on that slave” would be thought merciful “rather than most unnatural and barbarous” (Fourth Catilinarian 4.6.12); the case makes the senators feel, toward the conspirators, the anger of men whose own have been wronged. Cicero says of the poet Archias, “Should not I, then, love this man? should I not admire him?” (<em>For Archias</em> 18), so that the jurors will wish his good. And Pericles praises the Athenians for “acquiring our friends by conferring, not by receiving, favours” (Thucydides 2.40), which is kindness as Aristotle defines it.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “Every forger in the land is a thief of the public trust, and we should never be at peace with any of them.” Which passion does he chiefly arouse?',
      options:['Anger, because forgers have insulted the hearers','Hatred, because it is directed against a whole class','Kindness, because the speaker wishes to help the public','Love, because the hearers wish the public good'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Aristotle says that anger is felt toward particular individuals, whereas hatred is directed also against classes, as “we all hate any thief.” The speaker turns the hearers against every forger, so the passion is hatred.' } },
{ id:'fear-shame', src:['arist_rhet','cic_cat','thuc_crawley','plato_jowett'], pids:['cic-cat4-s6','thuc-nic-native','plato-ap-3'],
  h:'<p><strong>Fear, confidence, and shame</strong></p><p>We do not fear that the sun will burn out in a billion years, but we fear a storm that is coming tonight. A sailor who knows his ship is sound and the harbor near is confident in the same storm that frightens a passenger. And a student caught cheating may feel no fear of punishment at all and yet be ashamed before a teacher whose good opinion he values.</p><p>Aristotle defines these three as follows (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.5–6):</p><p><strong>Fear</strong>: “a pain or disturbance due to a mental picture of some destructive or painful evil in the future,” and only of evils that “appear not remote but so near as to be imminent.”<br><strong>Confidence</strong>: “the opposite of fear,” that is, “the expectation associated with a mental picture of the nearness of what keeps us safe and the absence or remoteness of what is terrible.”<br><strong>Shame</strong>: “pain or disturbance in regard to bad things, whether present, past, or future, which seem likely to involve us in discredit”; we feel it chiefly before those whose opinion we respect.</p><p>So a speaker who wants to frighten his hearers must make the danger seem near, and one who wants to give them confidence must make help seem near and the danger distant.</p><p>Cicero makes the danger near when he tells the senate that “The seeds of this evil have been carried further than you think; the contagion has not only spread through Italy, but it has crossed the Alps” (Fourth Catilinarian 4.3.6). Nicias, before the last battle in the harbor of Syracuse, gives his soldiers confidence by reminding them that “if our fleet wins the day, each can see his native city again,” and he tells them, “You must not lose heart” (Thucydides 7.61). Socrates rouses shame when he asks his fellow citizen whether he is not “ashamed of heaping up the greatest amount of money and honour and reputation, and caring so little about wisdom and truth” (Plato, <em>Apology</em> 29d).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A general tells his troops, “The enemy’s supplies are exhausted, our reinforcements are a day’s march away, and the river protects our flank.” Which passion is he chiefly arousing?',
      options:['Fear, because he speaks of the enemy','Shame, because he reminds them of their duty','Emulation, because he praises the reinforcements','Confidence, because help is near and danger far'],
      correct:3,
      explain:'Aristotle defines confidence as the expectation that what keeps us safe is near and what is terrible is absent or far. The general makes help seem near (the reinforcements, the river) and the danger weak, so he arouses confidence, not fear.' } },
{ id:'pity-indignation', src:['arist_rhet','thuc_crawley','cic_yonge','appown'], pids:['thuc-plat-compassion','cic-milo-105'],
  h:'<p><strong>Pity, indignation, and emulation</strong></p><p>When a hardworking family loses its farm in a flood, we feel pity. When a swindler grows rich and lives in luxury, we feel something else, which is not pity and is not quite anger, since he has not wronged us; we are indignant. And when a classmate of our own age and background wins a prize that we could have won, we may feel a pain that makes us work harder, without wishing that he had lost it.</p><p>Aristotle defines these three as follows (<em>Rhetoric</em> II.8–11):</p><p><strong>Pity</strong>: “a feeling of pain caused by the sight of some evil, destructive or painful, which befalls one who does not deserve it, and which we might expect to befall ourselves or some friend of ours.”<br><strong>Indignation</strong>: “pain caused by the sight of undeserved good fortune.” It differs from anger, because there need be no slight to ourselves, and it is the contrary of pity, which is pain at undeserved misfortune.<br><strong>Emulation</strong>: “pain caused by seeing the presence, in persons whose nature is like our own, of good things that are highly valued and are possible for ourselves to acquire; but it is felt not because others have these goods, but because we have not got them ourselves.” It differs from envy, because “Emulation makes us take steps to secure the good things in question,” whereas envy wishes the other not to have them.</p><p>Pity is sometimes confused with love, because both concern another’s good; but love wishes the good of the person, whereas pity is a pain at his undeserved suffering, and we may pity a stranger whom we do not love. The Plataeans, pleading for their lives before the Spartan judges, ask them “to yield to the impressions of a reasonable compassion; reflecting not merely on the awful fate in store for us, but also on the character of the sufferers, and on the impossibility of predicting how soon misfortune may fall even upon those who deserve it not” (Thucydides 3.59). They name exactly the conditions of Aristotle’s definition: the suffering is undeserved, and it could befall the judges themselves. Cicero ends his defense of Milo by saying that he cannot speak any longer “for weeping” (<em>Pro Milone</em> 105), so that the jurors will feel pity for a man about to be banished.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says of a corrupt official, “He has never done an honest day’s work, and now he rides in a carriage paid for by the poor.” Which passion does the speaker chiefly arouse?',
      options:['Pity, at the undeserved suffering of the poor','Emulation, at goods that the hearers could acquire','Indignation, at the undeserved good fortune of the official','Love, because the hearers wish the poor well'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Aristotle defines indignation as pain at the sight of undeserved good fortune. The speaker fixes the hearers’ attention on the official’s prosperity, which he has not earned, so the passion is indignation, which is the contrary of pity.' } },
{ id:'gregory', src:['greg'], pids:['greg-1'], taught:['greg:the joyful and the sad','greg:the poor and the rich'],
  h:'<p><strong>The same word does not suit every hearer</strong></p><p>A coach must say different things to two players on the same team. One is overconfident and has to be held back; the other is discouraged and has to be heartened. If the coach gave both the same speech, he would make one of them worse. A good teacher, a good doctor, and a good parent all know this, and they suit what they say to the person in front of them.</p><p>St. Gregory the Great makes this the principle of the third book of his <em>Pastoral Care</em>, written for bishops and others who must preach and admonish: “one and the same exhortation does not suit all, inasmuch as neither are all bound together by similarity of character. For the things that profit some often hurt others; seeing that also for the most part herbs which nourish some animals are fatal to others; and the gentle hissing that quiets horses incites whelps; and the medicine which abates one disease aggravates another” (III, Prologue). “Therefore,” he concludes, “according to the quality of the hearers ought the discourse of teachers to be fashioned, so as to suit all and each for their several needs.”</p><p>So Gregory proceeds by pairs of hearers, each pair being two kinds of people who need contrary words. The joyful and the sad, for example, are paired because the joyful must be warned of the sorrows that follow excess, whereas the sad must be heartened by the joys that are promised; and the poor and the rich are paired because the poor need comfort in tribulation, whereas the rich need to fear the pride that wealth brings. This is the doctrine of the hearer that the rest of rhetoric presupposes: Aristotle’s passions tell us how hearers are moved, and Gregory’s pairs tell us that hearers differ in what they need.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why, on Gregory’s principle, does a preacher speak differently to the timid and to the rash?',
      options:['Because the timid are more worthy of his attention than the rash','Because what heals one of them would harm the other','Because the rash cannot be taught at all, and the timid can','Because the same exhortation suits every hearer equally well'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Gregory says that “the things that profit some often hurt others,” as the medicine that abates one disease aggravates another. The timid need encouragement, which would make the rash worse, and the rash need restraint, which would crush the timid.' } },
{ id:'gregory-pairs', src:['greg'], pids:['greg-3','greg-4','greg-5'], taught:['greg:the young and the old','greg:the impudent and the bashful','greg:the gluttonous and the abstinent','greg:those who do not begin a good work and those who do not finish one'],
  h:'<p><strong>Gregory’s pairs, in order</strong></p><p>We have seen that Gregory proceeds by pairs of hearers. He gives many pairs in the third book of the <em>Pastoral Care</em> (chapters 1–35), and they are easier to remember when they are grouped by kind. The grouping below is the course’s own arrangement, but every pair is Gregory’s.</p><p>First, pairs that differ by <strong>state of life or condition</strong>: men and women; the young and the old; the poor and the rich; the joyful and the sad; subjects and prelates; servants and masters; the wise of this world and the dull; and those who prosper in their temporal wishes and those worn by adversity. Here the rule is that each condition brings its own danger. For example, the young are for the most part improved by severity of admonition, whereas the old are better disposed by kind remonstrance, since it is written, “Rebuke not an elder, but entreat him as a father.” Again, servants are to be admonished not to despise those over them, whereas masters are not to forget that they too have a Lord in heaven.</p><p>Second, pairs that differ by <strong>temperament</strong>: the impudent and the bashful; the forward and the faint-hearted; the impatient and the patient; the simple and the insincere; the slothful and the hasty; the meek and the passionate; the humble and the proud; the obstinate and the fickle; the silent and the talkative; and those who fear scourges and those whom scourges do not correct. Here the two err in contrary directions, by excess and by defect, so that the correction of one would push the other further into his fault: the impudent are corrected only by open rebuke, which would crush the bashful, who are better touched by a rebuke given as if in passing. Again, the obstinate, who think too well of their own judgment, are told, “Be not wise in your own conceits,” whereas the fickle, who think too little of it, are told, “Let us not be carried about with every wind of doctrine.”</p><p>Third, pairs that differ by <strong>virtues and vices</strong>: the gluttonous and the abstinent; the merciful and the envious; the peaceful and the brawlers; those who give of their own and those who seize what belongs to others; those who neither covet nor give, and those who give and still seize; sowers of strifes and peacemakers; those who misread the sacred law and those who understand it but speak it without humility; and those who preach and those who are silent in the office of preaching. Here even a virtue has its own danger. For example, the gluttonous are to fear the levity and lust that follow on excess, whereas the abstinent are to fear the pride that follows on restraint. Again, the merciful are to keep their good deeds from pride, whereas the envious are to see that another’s gift takes nothing from them.</p><p>Fourth, pairs that differ by <strong>the way of sinning</strong>: those who have known sins of the flesh and those who have not; those who weep for sins and still commit them, and those who leave their sins and do not weep; those who commend the wrong they do and those who blame a wrong and still commit it; those overcome by a sudden passion and those who sin on purpose; those who often commit small sins and those who seldom commit grievous ones; those who do not begin a good work and those who do not finish one; and those who do evil in private and good before men, and those who hide their good. Here the rule is that the same sin needs a different cure according to how it is committed: those who never begin a good work are to be shown that what they love instead is vain, whereas those who do not finish one undo what they had begun. Again, those overcome by a sudden passion are struck while the heart is unguarded, whereas those who sin on purpose, having chosen their guilt with deliberation, incur a stricter judgment.</p><p>In every group, then, the question is the same: what does each of the two hearers need, and why would the word that heals one wound the other?</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In which group does the course place Gregory’s pair of the slothful and the hasty?',
      options:['State of life or condition, like the rich and the poor','Temperament, because the two err in contrary directions','The way of sinning, like those who sin suddenly or on purpose','Virtues and vices, like the merciful and the envious'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Slowness and haste are contrary faults of temperament, one by defect and the other by excess, so the pair belongs with the impudent and the bashful and the obstinate and the fickle. It is not a difference of condition, and it does not concern how a sin is committed.' } }
]};

DECKS.taxis = {
  title:'Arrangement', sub:'Taxis (the parts of the oration)',
  sources:['cic_inv','arist_rhet','cic_cat','cic_yonge','lysias_lamb','thuc_crawley','eng_cer','lincoln_ev','plato_jowett'],
  panels:[
{ id:'offices', src:['cic_inv','arist_rhet','appown'],
  h:'<p><strong>The parts of a speech are offices</strong></p><p>A tenant writing to a landlord to ask for repairs will usually do several things in order. He opens politely, so that the landlord will read on with goodwill. He says what has happened: the roof leaked in the storm and the ceiling is stained. He says what he is asking for. He gives his reasons, such as the lease and the damage that will follow if nothing is done. He answers the objection he expects, that the stain is old. And he closes by asking again, briefly, for what he wants. No one taught him this order; it follows from what the letter has to do.</p><p>The rhetoricians name the parts of a speech in the same way, by what each part does. Aristotle says that “the only necessary parts of a speech are the Statement and the Argument,” and that a speech “cannot in any case have more than Introduction, Statement, Argument, and Epilogue” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.13). The Latin tradition, following Cicero’s <em>De inventione</em> (I.14.19), names six parts, each with its own duty:</p><p><strong>Exordium</strong> (the opening): to make the hearers attentive, teachable, and well-disposed.<br><strong>Narration</strong> (<span class="lat">narratio</span>): to state the facts briefly, clearly, and plausibly.<br><strong>Division</strong> (<span class="lat">partitio</span>): to set out the points in dispute and the order of taking them.<br><strong>Proof</strong> (<span class="lat">confirmatio</span>): to give one’s own arguments.<br><strong>Refutation</strong> (<span class="lat">reprehensio</span>): to answer the other side.<br><strong>Peroration</strong> (<span class="lat">peroratio</span>): to recapitulate, and to move.</p><p>Aristotle’s four and the Latin six do not disagree. Aristotle counts refutation as part of the argument, and he treats division together with the statement, whereas the Latin tradition names each duty separately.</p><p>Because each part is named by its duty, the six are best called offices, that is, duties that a part of the speech may perform. They are not a template that every speech must follow in the same order. A part may be missing, or very short, or scattered through the speech, if the case does not need it, and one passage may do the work of two offices at once. In the tenant’s letter, the refutation is a single sentence. We take the offices in turn.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why does the course call the six parts of the oration “offices”?',
      options:['Because each is named by a duty that a part may perform','Because the six must always come in the same order in every speech','Because only public officials may deliver all six parts of a speech','Because Aristotle names six parts, which the Latin writers reduced'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'Each part is named by what it does (to win attention, to state the facts, to prove, and so on), and a part may be short or missing if the case does not need it. The order is not fixed for every speech, and it is Aristotle who names fewer parts, not more.' } },
{ id:'exordium', src:['cic_inv','cic_yonge','lysias_lamb','eng_cer'], pids:['cic-arch-1','lys-19-fear','eng-jc-antony-bury'], taught:['taxis:eng-t1'],
  h:'<p><strong>The exordium</strong></p><p>A speaker at a school board meeting who opens by insulting the board will not be heard, however good his case; one who opens by saying, “I have three short points, and the third concerns your own budget,” will be heard at once. The opening of a speech prepares the hearers to receive the rest of it, and it does so by making them willing to listen, ready to understand, and well disposed toward the speaker.</p><p>Cicero defines the exordium as “an address bringing the mind of the hearer into a suitable state to receive the rest of the speech,” which happens “if it has rendered him well disposed towards the speaker, attentive, and willing to receive information” (<em>De inventione</em> I.15.20). He says that goodwill “is produced by dwelling on four topics”: the speaker’s own character, the adversaries’ character, the character of the judges, and the case itself (I.16.22). He also distinguishes two kinds of exordium: the <strong>direct opening</strong> (<span class="lat">principium</span>), “an address, in plain words, immediately rendering the hearer well disposed towards one, or inclined to receive information, or attentive”; and the <strong>subtle approach</strong> (<span class="lat">insinuatio</span>), “an address which employs a certain dissimulation, and which by a circuitous route as it were obscurely creeps into the affections of the hearer” (I.15.20). The subtle approach is needed when the hearers are hostile, because “if peace and good-will be openly sought for from those who are enemies to one, they not only are not obtained, but the hatred which they bear one is even inflamed and increased.”</p><p>Cicero’s defense of Archias opens directly, with the speaker’s own character: “If there is any natural ability in me, O judges,—and I know how slight that is,” any skill he has belongs first to Archias (<em>For Archias</em> 1). Lysias’s speaker wins goodwill from his own difficulty, since if he fails “not only I but my father besides will be held to be guilty” (Lysias 19.1). Shakespeare’s Antony, facing a crowd that has just cheered Caesar’s killers, uses the subtle approach: “I come to bury Caesar, not to praise him” (<em>Julius Caesar</em> III.2). He seems to grant what the crowd believes, and so he gains a hearing for the praise that follows.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker must address an assembly that is angry with him and his client. On Cicero’s account, which kind of exordium should he use?',
      options:['The direct opening, asking plainly for their goodwill','The subtle approach, which reaches goodwill indirectly','No exordium at all, since the hearers already know the case','A narration of the facts, placed before any exordium'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Cicero says that when the hearers are strongly hostile, an open request for goodwill inflames their hatred, so the speaker must use the subtle approach (insinuatio), which works its way into their favor indirectly.' } },
{ id:'narration-division', src:['cic_inv','cic_cat','lincoln_ev'], pids:['cic-cat3-s15','eng-lincoln-first-inaugural'],
  h:'<p><strong>Narration and division</strong></p><p>Someone telling a doctor about an injury says when and how it happened, in order, and leaves out what does not matter; a story that wanders or contradicts itself makes the doctor doubt it. And before a meeting begins, a good chairman says what is already agreed and what is still to be decided, so that no one wastes time on what nobody disputes.</p><p>These are the duties of narration and division. Cicero says that a narration should have three qualities, “that it should be brief, open, and probable” (<em>De inventione</em> I.20.28), that is, brief, clear, and plausible. It is not always needed: when “the whole affair is so clearly comprehended by the hearers” that telling it again does no good, “it is best to abstain from any narration altogether” (I.21.30). So when the facts are agreed by both sides and only their meaning is disputed, the narration shrinks, sometimes to a single sentence. A narration can also carry feeling, because the way the facts are told can make the hearers indignant or merciful before any argument begins. Cicero tells the people what the senate decreed after the arrests: “Lastly a solemn thanksgiving to the immortal gods for their exceptional favour was voted on my account, an honour which has never before been granted to any civil magistrate since the foundation of Rome” (Third Catilinarian 3.6.15); the plain report already fills the hearers with relief and gratitude.</p><p>The <strong>division</strong> (<span class="lat">partitio</span>) has two forms, according to Cicero. One “points out what are the particulars as to which one is in agreement with the opposite party, and also what remains in dispute”; the other is that in which the matters “on which we are about to speak” are “briefly arranged and pointed out,” so that the hearer knows when the speech will end (I.22.31). Lincoln uses the first form in his First Inaugural Address: “One section of our country believes slavery is right, and ought to be extended, while the other believes it is wrong, and ought not to be extended. This is the only substantial dispute.”</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In a trial for damage to a fence, both sides agree that the defendant’s cart struck it; they dispute only whether he was driving carelessly. On Cicero’s account, which office will be short?',
      options:['The peroration, since there is nothing left to feel','The proof, since there is nothing left to argue','The narration, since the facts are already agreed','The exordium, since the jurors are already attentive'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Cicero says that when the hearers already know the whole affair, the narration may be left out altogether. Here the facts are agreed, so the narration can be a sentence, while proof and refutation take up the dispute about carelessness.' } },
{ id:'proof-refutation', src:['cic_inv','eng_cer','cic_yonge'], pids:['eng-henry-stronger','cic-milo-59'],
  h:'<p><strong>Proof and refutation</strong></p><p>In any argument between two people, each does two things: he gives his own reasons, and he answers the other’s. A student who wants a later curfew says that he has kept every rule this year; when his parents reply that his friends are unreliable, he answers that the friends he will be with are the ones they already trust.</p><p>The proof (<span class="lat">confirmatio</span>) is where the speaker sets out his own arguments, drawn from the topics, and the refutation (<span class="lat">reprehensio</span>) is where he answers the other side. Cicero says that refutation “proceeds from the same source of invention which confirmation employs, because whatever the topics may be by means of which any statement can be confirmed, the very same may be used in order to invalidate it” (<em>De inventione</em> I.42.78). He names four ways of refuting an argument: “All argumentation is reprehended when anything ... of those positions which are assumed, is not granted, or if, though they are granted, it is denied that the conclusion legitimately follows from them, or if it is shown that the very kind of argumentation is faulty, or if in opposition to one form and reliable sort of argumentation another is employed which is equally firm and convincing” (I.42.79). That is, first, a premise is denied; second, the conclusion is denied to follow; third, the form of the argument is shown to be faulty; and fourth, an equally strong argument is set against it. A common way of showing that the conclusion does not follow is to draw from the opponent’s premises a consequence that is absurd.</p><p>Patrick Henry refutes those who say the colonies are too weak to resist by drawing the absurd consequence of delay: “They tell us, sir, that we are weak – unable to cope with so formidable an adversary. But when shall we be stronger? Will it be the next week, or the next year? Will it be when we are totally disarmed, and when a British guard shall be stationed in every house?” (Speech to the Second Virginia Convention, 1775). Cicero refutes the evidence against Milo by questions that show who produced it: “Who demanded that they should be examined? Appius. Who produced them? Appius” (<em>Pro Milone</em> 59). The evidence is not denied; it is shown to come from an interested party.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'An opponent argues that the bridge must be closed because it is old. The speaker replies, “By that reasoning we must close the cathedral, the town hall, and every house on Main Street.” Which way of refuting does he use?',
      options:['He denies that the bridge is old','He draws an absurd consequence from the reasoning','He sets an equally strong argument from expense against it','He shows that the opponent is an interested party'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The speaker grants that the bridge is old and shows that the opponent’s principle would lead to an absurd result, which shows that the conclusion does not follow from age alone. He does not deny the premise or bring in a new argument.' } },
{ id:'peroration', src:['cic_inv','cic_yonge','cic_cat'], pids:['cic-phil2-118','cic-cat4-s24'],
  h:'<p><strong>The peroration</strong></p><p>At the end of a long meeting, a good speaker reminds the hearers in a few words of the points he has made, because they will have forgotten some of them, and then he says why the decision matters. A lawyer’s closing argument does the same: it gathers what the jury has heard and leaves them with the feeling in which they should vote.</p><p>Cicero says that the conclusion of the speech “has three parts,—enumeration, indignation, and complaint” (<em>De inventione</em> I.52.98):</p><p><strong>Enumeration</strong> (the summing up): matters “which have been related in a scattered and diffuse manner are collected together, and, for the sake of recollecting them, are brought under our view.”<br><strong>Indignation</strong>: “a kind of speech by which the effect produced is, that great hatred is excited against a man, or great dislike of some proceeding is originated” (I.53.100).<br><strong>Complaint</strong>: “a speech seeking to move the pity of the hearers” (I.55.106).</p><p>He warns that the summing up should be varied, because if it is always done the same way, “it will be completely evident to every one that it is being handled according to some artificial system.”</p><p>Cicero ends the Fourth Catilinarian with a summing up that gathers everything at stake into one sentence, asking the senate to decide “as to your wives and children, as to your altars and hearths, your sanctuaries and temples, the buildings and homes of the whole city” (4.11.24). He ends the Second Philippic by setting his own constancy against Antony’s danger: “I defended the republic as a young man, I will not abandon it now that I am old. I scorned the sword of Catiline, I will not quail before yours” (2.118). In both the peroration moves the hearers as well as reminding them.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A defense lawyer closes by describing the defendant’s small children waiting at home and asking the jury to imagine their grief if their father is condemned. Which part of the peroration is this?',
      options:['Enumeration, the summing up of the points made','Indignation, which stirs hatred against the accuser','Complaint, which seeks to move the hearers to pity','Division, which lays out the points in dispute'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Cicero calls complaint “a speech seeking to move the pity of the hearers.” The lawyer does not gather his arguments or stir hatred against anyone, and division is not a part of the peroration.' } },
{ id:'catilinarian', src:['cic_cat'], pids:['cic-cat1-1','cic-cat1-4'], taught:['taxis:t1','taxis:t13'],
  h:'<p><strong>One speech, the six offices: the First Catilinarian</strong></p><p>We have seen the six offices one by one; in a real speech they are woven together, and one passage may do the work of more than one. Cicero delivered the First Catilinarian in November 63 BC, in a meeting of the senate at which Catiline himself was present. The speech is forensic in its charge, since it accuses Catiline of a conspiracy, but deliberative in what it asks, since Cicero wants the senate to act and Catiline to leave the city.</p><p>The offices can be traced as follows. First, the <strong>exordium</strong>: “How much further, Catilina, will you carry your abuse of our forbearance?” (1.1). Cicero makes the senators attentive by naming the man in the room, and by telling him that his plans are known. Second, the <strong>narration</strong>: “You were then, Catilina, at Laeca’s house that night; you divided Italy into districts; you decided to what quarter you wished each of your friends to proceed” (1.9). Here Cicero tells what was done that night, in order. Third, the <strong>proof</strong>: the same night is then pressed as evidence. Cicero tells the senate that he learned of the plan in time to keep the assassins from his door, so the narration, once told, becomes the ground of the charge; the night is told and then used to prove. Fourth, the <strong>division</strong>, which is implicit: the question is not whether there is a conspiracy, which Cicero treats as known, but what the senate and Catiline should now do. Fifth, the <strong>refutation</strong>: Cicero answers the objection that he has not put Catiline to death, explaining why he prefers that Catiline leave. Sixth, the <strong>peroration</strong> is a command, that Catiline leave the city, and with it an appeal to the fear and shame of the senate and of Catiline himself.</p><p>So the account of Laeca’s house is narration in the sentences that tell it, because they state what was done, and it becomes proof when Cicero presses the same night as evidence of the conspiracy.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In the First Catilinarian, what does the account of the night at Laeca’s house do?',
      options:['It is only the exordium, because it comes before every other part','It states what was done, and Cicero then uses it as evidence','It is the peroration, because Cicero commands Catiline to leave','It is the division, because it states what is agreed and disputed'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The sentences on Laeca’s house tell in order what was planned that night, which is narration; Cicero then presses the same night as evidence of the conspiracy, which is proof. The exordium is the opening questions, and the command to leave belongs to the close.' } },
{ id:'species-arrangement', src:['arist_rhet','thuc_crawley','plato_jowett'], pids:['thuc-fun-42','thuc-fun-44','plato-menex-237b'],
  h:'<p><strong>Arrangement fitted to the species</strong></p><p>A speech at a town meeting about a new school seldom needs a long story, since the question is what to do next year; a speech at a trial almost always needs one, since the question is what happened. A speech at a funeral needs neither a story of a crime nor an argument for a policy, but it does need an order: who the person was, what he did, and what comfort there is for those who remain.</p><p>Aristotle says that the arrangement depends on the species. “In political oratory there is very little opening for narration; nobody can ‘narrate’ what has not yet happened. If there is narration at all, it will be of past events, the recollection of which is to help the hearers to make better plans for the future” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.16). In epideictic speeches the facts are not told all at once but are spread through the praise: “Narration in ceremonial oratory is not continuous but intermittent” (III.16). Even a forensic speech may do without an epilogue if it is short.</p><p>The Greek funeral speech had an order of its own. Pericles’ speech over the Athenian dead moves, first, through the custom of the occasion; second, the ancestors who handed the land down free; third, the constitution and way of life of the city; fourth, the dead themselves, for whom “there is justice in the claim that steadfastness in his country’s battles should be as a cloak to cover a man’s other imperfections” (Thucydides 2.42); and, fifth, consolation for the living: “Comfort, therefore, not condolence, is what I have to offer to the parents of the dead who may be here” (2.44). Plato’s <em>Menexenus</em> follows a similar order, beginning with the birth of the dead from their own land, since their ancestors “were not strangers” but “children of the soil” (237b–c).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On Aristotle’s account, why does a deliberative speech seldom need a narration?',
      options:['Because nobody can narrate what has not yet happened','Because the assembly always knows every fact of the case','Because deliberative speakers are forbidden to tell stories','Because narration belongs only to the funeral speech'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'A deliberative speech is about the future, and Aristotle says that nobody can narrate what has not yet happened; if past events are told at all, it is to help the hearers plan. Narration belongs chiefly to forensic speeches, not to funeral speeches.' } }
]};

DECKS.lexis = {
  title:'Virtue of Style', sub:'Lexis (clarity, propriety, metaphor, the period)',
  sources:['arist_freese','arist_poet','gorgias_vh','thuc_crawley','eng_cer','eng_cem','lincoln_ev','cic_yonge','burke_n'],
  panels:[
{ id:'clear-fitting', src:['arist_freese','lincoln_ev'], pids:['eng-lincoln-war-came'],
  h:'<p><strong>Clear and fitting speech</strong></p><p>A waiter who calls a bowl of soup “a symphony of the sea” sounds false, and a doctor who tells a frightened patient that the results are “suboptimal” sounds evasive. In each case the words do not fit the thing or the occasion. On the other hand, a eulogy spoken in the words of a shopping list would fail too, because it falls below the dignity of the subject. We trust speech that is clear and that fits its matter, and we distrust speech that seems to be performing.</p><p>Aristotle makes clearness the first virtue of style: “one of its chief merits may be defined as perspicuity. This is shown by the fact that the speech, if it does not make the meaning clear, will not perform its proper function; neither must it be mean, nor above the dignity of the subject, but appropriate to it” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.2.1). Style, then, has two virtues: <strong>clearness</strong> (perspicuity), which comes chiefly from “the proper ones” among nouns and verbs, that is, the ordinary words for things (III.2.2), and <strong>propriety</strong>, which is fitness to the subject, the speaker, and the occasion. A little departure from ordinary words gives speech dignity, but the departure must not be noticed: “those who practise this artifice must conceal it and avoid the appearance of speaking artificially instead of naturally; for that which is natural persuades, but the artificial does not. For men become suspicious of one whom they think to be laying a trap for them” (III.2.4). For this reason prose should use “Proper and appropriate words and metaphors” (III.2.6), since these are what everyone uses in conversation.</p><p>Lincoln’s Second Inaugural Address shows how much plain words can do: “Both parties deprecated war, but one of them would make war rather than let the nation survive, and the other would accept war rather than let it perish, and the war came.” Every word is ordinary, and the clauses are balanced without calling attention to themselves; the last four words state the outcome as simply as a fact can be stated. Propriety, however, depends a great deal on the choice of each name.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why, on Aristotle’s account, should a speaker conceal the art of his style?',
      options:['Because hearers distrust a style that seems artificial','Because ornament of every kind is out of place in a speech in prose','Because clearness requires that only strange words be used','Because the art of style cannot be learned at all'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'Aristotle says that what is natural persuades, whereas the artificial does not, because men become suspicious of one who seems to be laying a trap for them. He allows ordinary words and metaphors in prose, and he does not say that style cannot be learned.' } },
{ id:'name', src:['arist_freese','appown'],
  h:'<p><strong>Choosing the name</strong></p><p>The same car can be advertised as “pre-owned” or sold as “used,” and the same official can be praised as “a public servant” or attacked as “a bureaucrat.” A candidate’s supporters speak of his “firmness,” and his opponents of his “stubbornness.” The thing named is the same, but each name makes it appear better or worse, and the hearers’ judgment follows the name.</p><p>Aristotle teaches the speaker to choose the name deliberately: “if we wish to ornament our subject, we must derive our metaphor from the better species under the same genus; if to depreciate it, from the worse” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.2.10). That is, of two names for things of the same kind, the speaker who praises takes the nobler and the speaker who blames takes the meaner. Epithets work in the same way, since they “may be applied from what is vile or disgraceful” or “from what is more honorable” (III.2.14), and so do diminutives, since “It is the diminutive which makes the good and the bad appear less” (III.2.15): to call a rival’s plan “his little scheme” makes it seem small.</p><p>Aristotle adds three cautions. First, the sound of a word matters, and “Metaphors should also be derived from things that are beautiful, the beauty of a word consisting ... in its sound or sense, and its ugliness in the same” (III.2.13). Second, a metaphor that gives a name to something that has none should come “from what is akin and of the same kind, so that, as soon as it is uttered, it is clearly seen to be akin” (III.2.12), as we speak of the “mouth” of a river or the “foot” of a mountain. Third, the speaker “must be careful to observe the due mean” in diminutives and epithets (III.2.15), since too many of them make the trick obvious. Some faults of style, however, come from too much of what is otherwise good.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker who wishes to praise a general calls his retreat “a strategic withdrawal.” What is he doing, on Aristotle’s account?',
      options:['Choosing the nobler of two names for the same thing','Using a diminutive to make the retreat appear less','Using a strange word to give his style a foreign air','Giving a name to a thing that had no name before it'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'A retreat and a strategic withdrawal are names for the same kind of movement, and the speaker chooses the nobler one in order to praise. A diminutive makes a thing seem small, and the retreat already had a name.' } },
{ id:'frigid', src:['arist_freese','appown'],
  h:'<p><strong>Frigid style</strong></p><p>Some writing tries so hard to be impressive that it leaves the reader cold. A travel brochure that speaks of “the azure-watered, sun-drenched, palm-fringed shore” tires us after one sentence, and a speech that calls every problem “a towering inferno of crisis” stops being believed. Such style is what the rhetoricians call <strong>frigid</strong> (Greek <span class="lat">psychros</span>, cold), because it aims at warmth and grandeur and produces neither.</p><p>Aristotle names four causes of it (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.3):</p><p><strong>Compound words</strong>: “the use of compound words,” such as “sun-drenched” and “palm-fringed,” which belong to poetry (III.3.1).<br><strong>Strange words</strong>: “the use of strange words” (III.3.2), that is, rare, foreign, or archaic words where ordinary ones would serve.<br><strong>Epithets</strong>: “the use of epithets that are either long or unseasonable or too crowded” (III.3.3); a speaker who never says “the sea” but always “the wide and restless sea” uses epithets, as Aristotle says of one writer, “not as a seasoning but as a regular dish.”<br><strong>Inappropriate metaphors</strong>: metaphors that are “ridiculous,” or “too dignified and somewhat tragic,” or “farfetched,” in which case “they are obscure” (III.3.4).</p><p>Aristotle grants that epithets may be used “to a certain extent, since it removes the style from the ordinary and gives a foreign air. But one must aim at the mean, for neglect to do so does more harm than speaking at random; for a random style lacks merit, but excess is vicious” (III.3.3).</p><p>All four causes have the same root: the speaker uses in prose what belongs to poetry, so the art becomes visible and the hearer stops believing. Of the four, the metaphor deserves the most attention, because it can be the greatest virtue of style as well as a fault.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker calls a modest school budget “the mighty and ravenous leviathan of fiscal ruin.” Which cause of frigid style does the phrase chiefly show?',
      options:['Compound words, which join two words into one','A metaphor too tragic for its subject','A diminutive that makes the budget appear smaller','A proper word used in its ordinary sense'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Calling a modest budget a leviathan of ruin is a metaphor far too grand and tragic for the subject. The phrase has no compound word, and nothing in it makes the budget appear smaller.' } },
{ id:'metaphor', src:['arist_freese','arist_poet','eng_cer'], pids:['eng-johnson-farthing-candle'], taught:['fig:eng-johnson-farthing-candle:metaphor'],
  h:'<p><strong>Metaphor</strong></p><p>Ordinary speech is full of metaphors that we no longer notice: “the leg of a table,” “a cold reception,” “the heart of the matter,” “a bottleneck in traffic.” Each takes a word from one thing and gives it to another because of a likeness, and each says something quickly that would take many words to say plainly. A good new metaphor does more: it makes us see a likeness we had not seen before.</p><p>Aristotle defines metaphor in the <em>Poetics</em>: “Metaphor is the application of an alien name by transference either from genus to species, or from species to genus, or from species to species, or by analogy, that is, proportion” (<em>Poetics</em> 21). The metaphor from proportion takes four terms: as A is to B, so C is to D, and then C is called “the D of A.” So we call the beginning of a nation its “dawn,” because the beginning is to the nation as dawn is to the day. Such metaphors should work in both directions, “reciprocal and applicable to either of the two things of the same genus” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.4.4); if youth is the spring of life, then spring may be called the youth of the year. Of the kinds of metaphor, Aristotle says, “the most popular are those based on proportion” (III.10.7).</p><p>He also explains why metaphor pleases: “Easy learning is naturally pleasant to all, and words mean something, so that all words which make us learn something are most pleasant. Now we do not know the meaning of strange words, and proper terms we know already. It is metaphor, therefore, that above all produces this effect” (III.10.2). For this reason a metaphor must be “neither strange, for then it is difficult to take in at a glance, nor superficial, for then it does not impress the hearer” (III.10.6). And in the <em>Poetics</em> he says: “But the greatest thing by far is to have a command of metaphor” (22).</p><p>Samuel Johnson, asked about Thomas Sheridan’s plan to fix English pronunciation, replied, “Sir, it is burning a farthing candle at Dover to show light at Calais” (Boswell, <em>Life of Johnson</em>). The metaphor is from proportion: the man’s efforts are to the language as a tiny candle is to the distance across the Channel. Metaphor has a near relative in the simile.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Someone calls old age “the evening of life.” On which kind of metaphor does the phrase rest?',
      options:['From genus to species, since evening is a kind of life','From proportion: age is to life as evening to day','A strange word, which the hearers do not understand','A compound word, which belongs only to poetry'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The phrase takes four terms in proportion: old age is to life as evening is to the day, so old age is called the evening of life. Evening is not a kind of life, and the words are neither strange nor compound.' } },
{ id:'simile', src:['arist_freese','eng_cer'], pids:['eng-bacon-fame-river'], taught:['fig:eng-bacon-fame-river:simile'],
  h:'<p><strong>Simile and metaphor</strong></p><p>“He fought like a lion” and “He was a lion in the fight” say almost the same thing. The first states the likeness openly, with the word “like,” and the second takes it for granted and calls the man a lion. The first is easier to follow; the second is quicker and more striking.</p><p>Aristotle calls the first a <strong>simile</strong> and treats it as a kind of metaphor: “The simile also is a metaphor; for there is very little difference” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.4.1). It is “a metaphor differing only by the addition of a word, wherefore it is less pleasant because it is longer; it does not say that this is that, so that the mind does not even examine this” (III.10.3). So the metaphor asks more of the hearer and gives more pleasure when he understands it, whereas the simile explains itself. Aristotle also warns that the simile belongs more to poetry: it “is also useful in prose, but should be less frequently used, for there is something poetical about it” (III.4.2). A simile in prose, then, should be short and plainly useful.</p><p>Francis Bacon gives a simile that also argues: “Certainly fame is like a river, that beareth up things light and swollen, and drowns things weighty and solid” (<em>Of Praise</em>, 1625). The likeness is stated openly (“like a river”), and the rest of the sentence draws out the point of it: fame, like a river, carries what is light and sinks what is solid. A simile and a metaphor both make us see a likeness, but some figures make us see the thing itself.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why, on Aristotle’s account, does a simile please less than a metaphor?',
      options:['Because it is longer and does not say that this is that','Because it uses strange words that the hearer does not know','Because similes belong only to tragedy and never to prose','Because a simile states a likeness that is not true'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'Aristotle says that the simile adds a word and so is longer, and because it does not say “this is that,” the mind does not examine it. He allows similes in prose, though less often, and he does not say that their likenesses are false.' } },
{ id:'before-eyes', src:['arist_freese','cic_yonge','eng_cer'], pids:['cic-phil2-112','eng-richard3-horse'], taught:['fig:eng-richard3-horse:hyperbole'],
  h:'<p><strong>Setting things before the eyes</strong></p><p>“The economy is weak” tells us something; “the shops on Main Street stand empty, with their windows soaped over” lets us see it. A good storyteller makes the listener feel present at what is told. Some sayings please us in another way, by turning out differently from what we expected, so that we learn something in the moment of surprise.</p><p>Aristotle says that the speaker should aim at “three things—metaphor, antithesis, actuality” (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.10.6). By actuality he means vividness: “things are set before the eyes by words that signify actuality” (III.11.2), that is, words that show a thing in action. One way of doing this is to speak “of inanimate things as if they were animate” (III.11.3), as when we say that a storm “attacked” the coast. He names three other sources of such sayings:</p><p><strong>Surprise</strong>: a saying pleases when “the conclusion turns out contrary to his expectation, and the mind seems to say, How true it is! but I missed it” (III.11.6).<br><strong>Conciseness and antithesis</strong>: “the more concisely and antithetically they are expressed, the greater is their popularity. The reason is that antithesis is more instructive and conciseness gives knowledge more rapidly” (III.11.9).<br><strong>Hyperbole</strong>: “Approved hyperboles are also metaphors” (III.11.15), that is, they say more than is literally true in order to show how great a thing is; but “There is something youthful about hyperboles; for they show vehemence” (III.11.15–16), and so they suit a speaker in passion more than a calm one.</p><p>Cicero sets the moment itself before the senate’s eyes when he challenges Antony: “But this single day, this very day that now is, this very moment while I am speaking, defend your conduct during this very moment, if you can” (<em>Philippics</em> 2.112). Shakespeare’s Richard, unhorsed in battle, cries, “A horse! a horse! my kingdom for a horse!” (<em>Richard III</em> V.4). No one would literally trade a kingdom for a horse, and the hyperbole shows the vehemence of his need.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says of a long sermon, “I grew a beard while he preached.” What is this, on Aristotle’s account, and what does it show?',
      options:['A simile, which states a likeness openly','A hyperbole, which shows vehemence by excess','A proper term, which says exactly what happened','A diminutive, which makes the sermon seem less'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'No one grows a beard during a sermon, so the speaker says more than is true to show how long it seemed; that is a hyperbole, which Aristotle says shows vehemence. There is no “like,” and the words do not make the sermon seem small.' } },
{ id:'style-argument', src:['gorgias_vh','thuc_crawley','arist_freese'], pids:['gor-hel-8','thuc-diod-1','thuc-per-3'], taught:['lexis:lx1','lexis:lx2'],
  h:'<p><strong>When style takes the place of argument</strong></p><p>A slogan can make a weak claim sound strong. “No pain, no gain” sounds like a law of nature, but it does not tell us which pains bring gain and which do not. When a sentence is admired chiefly for its sound and balance, it is fair to ask what it would claim if it were said in plain words, and whether that claim would still be believed.</p><p>The rhetorical tradition asks this question of Gorgias. His <em>Encomium of Helen</em> is almost all figure: antithesis, equal members, and like endings, which Van Hook’s English keeps. Gorgias says of speech that it “is a powerful potentate, who with frailest, feeblest frame works wonders.” Here the figure is the claim: speech is small and does what a ruler does. If we remove the personification and the contrast of frail frame and great wonders, little argument remains. Aristotle admires the power of such style while he distrusts its use, since he holds “that which is natural persuades, but the artificial does not,” and Gorgias’s art is meant to be seen. Diodotus, by contrast, says in the debate on Mytilene: “I think the two things most opposed to good counsel are haste and passion” (Thucydides 3.42). We may keep or drop the balance of the two members, and the claim still stands, and it can be argued for or against. That is style serving an argument, not replacing one.</p><p>Pericles’ funeral speech shows that elaborate balance need not empty a sentence of argument: “We cultivate refinement without extravagance and knowledge without effeminacy; wealth we employ more for use than for show” (Thucydides 2.40). Said plainly, the claim is that the Athenians love beauty and learning without being made soft by them, and use their wealth for practical ends; that is a claim about the city that can be judged true or false as praise. So the test is the same in every case: we restate the sentence in plain clauses and ask what remains. The balance of clauses on which much of this style depends has its own doctrine, the doctrine of the period.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A campaign poster reads, “Strong hands, strong land.” When we restate it in plain clauses, what do we find?',
      options:['A full argument that the candidate’s policies will strengthen the country','A claim that depends on the repetition, since no reason is given','A narration of the facts of the candidate’s career','A metaphor from proportion that teaches a likeness'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'In plain words the poster says only that the candidate is strong and the country will be strong, with no reason to connect the two; the repetition of “strong” does the work that an argument should do. It tells no facts, and it is not a metaphor from proportion.' } },
{ id:'period', src:['arist_freese','burke_n','appown'], pids:['eng-burke-tranquillity'],
  h:'<p><strong>The periodic style</strong></p><p>A speaker who strings clause after clause with “and” and “and then” tires us, because we cannot tell when he will stop; a speaker whose sentences each come round to a close lets us rest at the end of each and take in what was said. We prefer the second, even when the first tells us more.</p><p>Aristotle distinguishes two styles (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.9). The <strong>continuous style</strong> is “that which has no end in itself and only stops when the sense is complete. It is unpleasant, because it is endless, for all wish to have the end in sight” (III.9.2). The <strong>periodic style</strong> is made of periods, and “by period I mean a sentence that has a beginning and end in itself and a magnitude that can be easily grasped” (III.9.3). It is pleasant and easy to remember, because “the periodic style has number,” as verse has. Several rules follow:</p><p><strong>The period ends with the sense</strong>: “the period must be completed with the sense and not stop short” (III.9.4), or the hearer may take a false meaning from a pause.<br><strong>Clauses neither too short nor too long</strong>: “neither clauses nor periods should be curtailed or too long” (III.9.6).<br><strong>Divided and opposed clauses</strong>: “The clauses of the periodic style are divided or opposed” (III.9.7). Divided clauses are distinct members set side by side; in opposed clauses “one contrary is brought close to another.”<br><strong>Antithesis</strong>: the opposed style pleases “because contraries are easily understood and even more so when placed side by side, and also because antithesis resembles a syllogism; for refutation is a bringing together of contraries” (III.9.8).<br><strong>Like endings</strong>: clauses may also be made equal in length or alike in their final syllables (III.9.9).</p><p>Burke uses opposed clauses to state his whole policy toward the American colonies in one period: “I am not determining a point of law, I am restoring tranquillity; and the general character and situation of a people must determine what sort of government is fitted for them.” The first two clauses set “determining a point of law” against “restoring tranquillity,” and the third gives the reason for preferring the second. The modern periodic sentence, which holds its main clause until the end, depends on the same waiting for the end, and it is treated among the choices between correct sentences.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “We came as strangers; we leave as friends.” Of which kind are the clauses, on Aristotle’s division?',
      options:['Divided, since the clauses are distinct but set no contraries','Opposed, since contraries stand side by side','Continuous, since the sentence has no end in itself','Curtailed, since each clause is too short to be a period'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'“Came” and “leave,” “strangers” and “friends” are contraries brought close to each other in the two clauses, so the clauses are opposed. The sentence has a clear end, and its clauses, though short, complete the sense.' } }
]};


DECKS.effects = {
  title:'Choosing among Correct Sentences', sub:'Order, voice, and emphasis in the sentence',
  sources:['appown','quint','arist_freese','cic_orat'],
  panels:[
{ src:['appown'], h:'<p><strong>Several correct sentences.</strong> English usually allows us to say one thing in several correct ways. We can write <em>The council approved the plan</em> or <em>The plan was approved by the council</em>, and we can write <em>The storm came at night</em> or <em>At night the storm came</em>. Each version is grammatical, and each says the same thing. But the versions differ in order, in voice, or in construction, and so they do not fit every passage equally well.</p><p>The first principle concerns <strong>known and new information</strong>. A sentence usually begins with what the reader already knows from the sentences before it, and it puts the new information toward the end. For example, after <em>Last year the museum bought a painting by an unknown artist</em>, the sentence <em>The painting shows a sailor on the deck of a ship</em> begins with the painting, which the reader knows, and ends with the sailor, who is new. The version <em>A sailor on the deck of a ship is shown in the painting</em> says the same thing, but it begins with the new information, so the reader has to wait to learn how it connects with the sentence before.</p>' },
{ src:['quint','appown'], h:'<p><strong>End focus.</strong> The end of a sentence is the place of greatest weight, so we usually put there the word or phrase that we most want the reader to notice. Quintilian says that “It is, however, not infrequently possible to give special significance to a word by placing it at the close of the sentence and thereby stamping and impressing it on the mind of the hearer, whereas if it were placed in the middle of the sentence, it would remain unnoticed, escape the attention and be obscured by its surroundings” (IX.4.29). He also says, “If the demands of artistic structure permit, it is far best to end the sentence with a verb” (IX.4.26). He is writing about Latin, which can put the verb last more easily than English can, but the principle of the weighted ending holds in both languages.</p><p>For example, after <em>The guests expected a speech from the mayor</em>, the sentence <em>The speech, as it turned out, came from her ten-year-old son</em> ends on the surprise. The version <em>Her ten-year-old son, as it turned out, gave the speech</em> ends on the speech, which the reader already expected.</p>' },
{ src:['appown'], h:'<p><strong>Cohesion and the passive.</strong> A paragraph is easier to follow when its sentences keep the same subject, because the reader can then follow one topic from sentence to sentence. The passive voice (in which the subject is the thing acted on, as in <em>the bridge was built</em>) is one way to keep that subject. After <em>The old bridge is the pride of the town</em>, the sentence <em>It was built in 1820 by a Scottish mason</em> keeps the bridge as the subject, but <em>A Scottish mason built it in 1820</em> turns the paragraph toward the mason.</p><p>The passive also moves the doer of the action to the end of the sentence, where it receives the most weight, as <em>by a Scottish mason</em> does in the first version. But the passive is not better in every case, because when the doer is the topic of the paragraph, the active voice keeps the doer as the subject.</p>' },
{ src:['appown'], h:'<p><strong>The cleft sentence.</strong> A cleft sentence divides one statement into two clauses, and so it singles out one part of the statement for emphasis. The it-cleft puts the stressed part after <em>It was</em> or <em>It is</em>, so <em>It was the gardener who found the key</em> gives the most weight to the gardener, and <em>It was the key that the gardener found</em> gives the most weight to the key. The what-cleft puts the stressed part at the end, as in <em>What the gardener found was the key</em>.</p><p>The cleft is most useful when the reader expects something else, for example when everyone has supposed that the cook found the key. The there-construction (<em>There was a key under the mat</em>) works in a similar way, because it lets a new subject come after the verb instead of at the head of the sentence.</p>' },
{ src:['arist_freese','cic_orat','quint'], h:'<p><strong>Rhythm and the period.</strong> In a series we usually put the shorter members before the longer, so that the sentence grows toward its end. For example, <em>We need time, money, and the patience to wait for results</em> is easier to follow than <em>We need the patience to wait for results, money, and time</em>. Quintilian gives a similar rule for force, because “sentences rise and grow in force”, and so a weaker word should not follow a stronger one (IX.4.23).</p><p>A periodic sentence holds its main clause until the end, so that the sense is complete only when the sentence closes. <em>When the last guest had gone and the lamps were out, she opened the letter</em> is periodic, but <em>She opened the letter when the last guest had gone and the lamps were out</em> is loose, because its main clause comes first. Aristotle defines the period as “a sentence that has a beginning and end in itself and a magnitude that can be easily grasped”, and he says that the continuous style, which has no end in itself, is unpleasant, “for all wish to have the end in sight” (Rhetoric III.9.2–3). His period is a sentence rounded off and complete in itself, which is not quite the same as the modern periodic sentence, but both depend on the hearer’s waiting for the end. Cicero says that “men\'s ears are always on the watch for the end of a sentence” (Orator 199), and that the words at the beginning and in the middle of the sentence “ought to be composed with reference to the end” (Orator 200).</p>' }
]};

DECKS.whole = {
  title:'Reading the Whole Case', sub:'The parts of the art together',
  sources:['arist_rhet','cic_inv','thuc_crawley','sallust_w','demosth','antiphon'],
  panels:[
{ id:'in-order', src:['arist_rhet','demosth','appown'], pids:['dem-rhod-1'], taught:['species:dem-rhod-1','pistis:dem-rhod-1','ethos:dem-rhod-1'],
  h:'<p><strong>Taking a case in order</strong></p><p>A doctor examining a patient goes through the same questions every time, in the same order, so that nothing is missed: what brought the patient in, where it hurts, how long it has lasted, what has been tried. A reader of a speech can do the same. We have studied the parts of the art one by one, and a real speech uses them all at once; so it helps to ask the questions in an order that follows the art itself.</p><p>The questions can be taken in this order:</p><p><strong>The situation and the hearers</strong>: who is speaking, to whom, and on what occasion?<br><strong>The species and its end</strong>: are the hearers asked to judge a past act, to decide a future course, or to judge a person praised or blamed? So the end is to accuse or defend, to exhort or dissuade, or to praise or blame.<br><strong>The issue</strong>: in a forensic case, on which point does the dispute stand?<br><strong>The chief means of persuasion</strong>: does the passage work chiefly through the speaker’s character, the hearers’ feeling, or the argument?<br><strong>The office</strong>: which part of the speech is this, and what is it doing there?<br><strong>The figure</strong>: is there a figure in the passage, and what does it do for the argument?</p><p>Demosthenes opens his speech <em>For the Freedom of the Rhodians</em> in this way: “It is, I think, your duty, men of Athens, when you are deliberating upon affairs of such importance, to grant freedom of speech to every one of your advisers. And for my part, I have never yet felt any difficulty in pointing out to you the best course; for I believe that, broadly speaking, you all know from the first what this is. My difficulty is to persuade you to act upon your knowledge” (15.1). The situation is the Athenian assembly, which must decide whether to help the democrats of Rhodes. The speech is deliberative, since the assembly is “deliberating,” and its end is to exhort. Since the case is not a trial, there is no issue in the forensic sense. The passage works chiefly through ethos: the speaker shows practical wisdom and frankness, and he even compliments the hearers by saying that they already know the best course. It is the exordium, and it makes the hearers attentive by naming the real difficulty, which is not knowledge but action. No figure is prominent; the plain style suits a speaker who claims only to tell the truth. In paired speeches, these questions are asked of two voices at once.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In what order does the course take the questions about a whole case?',
      options:['The figure first, then the office, then the species','The situation, the species and end, the issue, the chief means, the office, the figure','The issue first, then the figure, then the situation','The chief means first, since it settles every other question'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The questions follow the art: first who speaks to whom and on what occasion, then what the hearers are asked to judge, then the issue, the chief means of persuasion, the office of the passage, and last the figure. The figure is named last because it is worth naming only for what it does in the case.' } },
{ id:'paired', src:['thuc_crawley','appown'],
  h:'<p><strong>Paired speeches</strong></p><p>At a town meeting, two neighbors may speak on the same question, one for the new road and one against it. Each makes a claim, gives reasons for it, and tries to make the hearers feel as he does; and the hearers judge best when they can set the two speeches side by side and see where they meet and where they part.</p><p>The Greek and Roman historians often present a debate in exactly this form, as two speeches on one question. Thucydides explains how he wrote them: “it was in all cases difficult to carry them word for word in one’s memory, so my habit has been to make the speakers say what was in my opinion demanded of them by the various occasions, of course adhering as closely as possible to the general sense of what they really said” (1.22). The paired speeches are therefore both history and models of argument, since the historian gives each side its strongest case.</p><p>To read a voice in such a pair, we ask three questions. First, what is its <strong>claim</strong>, that is, what does it want the hearers to decide, or, in a speech of praise, what does it praise? Second, what are its <strong>reasons</strong>? Third, what <strong>passion</strong> does it try to rouse or to calm? The claim must be stated as a decision (“Athens should keep the decree”), not as a reason (“because a democracy that changes its mind cannot rule an empire”), because two voices may share a reason and differ in what they ask. An epideictic voice asks for no decision, so its claim is what it praises or blames. Aristotle says that “Praise is the expression in words of the eminence of a man’s good qualities, and therefore we must display his actions as the product of such qualities” (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.9); the claim of a speech of praise is therefore that this person, or this city, is excellent in these ways. The claim of one voice is often clearest when it is set against the other, since each answers the other’s reasons. So the first step in reading a pair is to know the situation in which both speak.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In reading a voice in a paired debate, what is its claim?',
      options:['The passion that the speaker tries to rouse in the hearers','The decision that the speaker wants the hearers to make','The reason that the speaker gives for his decision','The species of the debate, which the two voices share'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The claim is what the voice wants the hearers to decide; in a speech of praise, which asks no decision, it is what the voice praises. The reasons support the claim, and the passion is what the speaker tries to rouse; two voices may share a reason and still make opposite claims.' } },
{ id:'ten-debates', src:['thuc_crawley','sallust_w','antiphon'],
  h:'<p><strong>The ten debates in brief</strong></p><p>We have seen that the first step in reading a pair of speeches is to know the situation. These are the situations of the ten debates in the course, in the order of events where there is one:</p><p><strong>Corcyra and Corinth at Athens</strong> (433 BC): Corcyra, at war with its mother city Corinth, asks Athens for an alliance, and Corinth asks Athens to refuse it (Thucydides 1.32–43).<br><strong>Archidamus and Sthenelaidas</strong> (432 BC): the Spartan assembly decides whether Athens has broken the peace and whether Sparta should go to war; the old king speaks first, and the ephor answers him (1.80–86).<br><strong>Pericles’ funeral oration</strong> (431–430 BC): at the public burial of those who fell in the first year of the war, Pericles is chosen to speak over the dead (2.35–46).<br><strong>Mytilene</strong> (427 BC): Athens has voted to put to death the men of Mytilene, which had revolted; the next day the assembly meets again, and Cleon and Diodotus speak on whether to keep the decree (3.37–48).<br><strong>Plataea before the Spartan judges</strong> (427 BC): after the surrender of Plataea, the Plataeans plead for their lives, and the Thebans answer them (3.53–67).<br><strong>Nicias and Alcibiades</strong> (415 BC): the Athenian assembly debates the great expedition to Sicily, which Nicias opposes and Alcibiades urges (6.9–18).<br><strong>Hermocrates and Euphemus at Camarina</strong> (415–414 BC): the Sicilian city of Camarina hears an envoy of Syracuse and an envoy of Athens, each asking it to take its side (6.76–87).<br><strong>Catiline to his followers, and Caesar on the penalty</strong> (63 BC): in Sallust’s account, Catiline urges his fellow conspirators on, and Caesar later speaks in the senate on how the arrested conspirators should be punished (Sallust, <em>Conspiracy of Catiline</em> 20, 51).<br><strong>Caesar and Cato on the prisoners</strong> (63 BC): in the same debate in the senate, Cato answers Caesar (51–52).<br><strong>Antiphon’s two sides</strong>: in a school case set in Athens, a father prosecutes the young man whose javelin killed his son at practice, and the young man’s father answers for him (Second Tetralogy).</p><p>Most of these debates are deliberative, since an assembly or a senate must decide what to do; but the judges at Plataea sit in a kind of trial, the funeral speech praises, and Antiphon’s case is argued before a jury, so the species must be judged in each case from what the hearers are asked to decide.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why must the species of each debate be judged from the situation, and not assumed?',
      options:['Because the historians give no situation for any of them','Because some debates decide policy, some judge, and one praises','Because every debate in the course is forensic, whatever its setting','Because the species depends only on the length of the speeches'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Most of the debates are deliberative, but the Plataean debate is argued before judges, Antiphon’s case before a jury, and the funeral speech is praise. So the species follows from what the hearers are asked to decide in each situation.' } }
]};

DECKS.figures = {
  title:'The Figures', sub:'Figures of speech and of thought, and the tropes',
  sources:['quint','eng_cer','eng_cem','cic_cat','cic_yonge','thuc_crawley','kjv'],
  panels:[
{ id:'what-figure', src:['quint','eng_cer','appown'], pids:['eng-jc-brutus-rome'], taught:['fig:eng-jc-brutus-rome:antithesis'],
  h:'<p><strong>What a figure is, and which figures the course asks about</strong></p><p>Brutus could have told the Roman crowd, “I loved Rome more than I loved Caesar.” Shakespeare has him say instead, “Not that I loved Caesar less, but that I loved Rome more” (<em>Julius Caesar</em> III.2). We notice the second sentence and remember it, because it departs from the plain way of saying the same thing: it sets two clauses side by side, in the same shape, with “less” against “more” and “Caesar” against “Rome.” Such a departure, made on purpose and for an effect, is what the rhetoricians call a figure.</p><p>Quintilian distinguishes two kinds of departure (<em>Institutio oratoria</em> IX.1.4). A <strong>trope</strong> (Greek <span class="lat">tropos</span>, a turn) changes the meaning of a word: its name “is applied to the transference of expressions from their natural and principal signification to another, with a view to the embellishment of style,” as in metaphor. A <strong>figure</strong> (Latin <span class="lat">figura</span>, shape) changes the shape of the sentence or of the thought while the words keep their meaning: it “is the term employed when we give our language a conformation other than the obvious and ordinary.” The tradition divides the figures further into figures of words, which lie in the arrangement of the words, and figures of thought, which lie in what the speaker does with the thought, such as asking a question he does not mean to have answered.</p><p>The course asks chiefly about eighteen figures and tropes that are common in the speeches. In the course’s own grouping, they are these:</p><p><strong>Repetition</strong>: anaphora.<br><strong>Balance and rising order</strong>: antithesis, isocolon, tricolon, climax, chiasmus, homoeoteleuton.<br><strong>Omission</strong>: asyndeton.<br><strong>Tropes</strong>: metaphor, simile, personification, hyperbole, litotes, irony.<br><strong>Figures of thought</strong>: rhetorical question, apostrophe, praeteritio, sententia.</p><p>Twenty-three further figures, such as epistrophe, polysyndeton, correctio, and aposiopesis, are also marked in the passages, but they are optional; the main figure sets do not ask about them, and an optional set at the end of Division VI does. A figure is worth naming only when we can say what it does in the passage, and the figures are easier to see in their groups.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On Quintilian’s distinction, why is metaphor a trope rather than a figure?',
      options:['Because it changes the meaning of a word','Because it always repeats the same word at the head of each member','Because it is found only in poetry and never in a speech','Because it asks a question that is not meant to be answered'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'A trope transfers a word from its proper meaning to another, as metaphor does; a figure gives the language an unusual shape while the words keep their meaning. Repetition and the rhetorical question are figures, and metaphor is common in prose.' } },
{ id:'repetition', src:['cic_cat','appown'], pids:['cic-cat4-1'], taught:['fig:cic-cat4-1:anaphora'],
  h:'<p><strong>Figures of repetition</strong></p><p>A coach tells a team, “We trained for this. We waited for this. We earned this.” The repeated words bind the three sentences together and make the hearer wait for the next one. Repetition is the simplest of the figures and one of the strongest, because the ear recognizes a returning word at once.</p><p>The core figure of this group is <strong>anaphora</strong>: the same word at the head of successive members. Cicero opens the Fourth Catilinarian with it: “I perceive, conscript fathers, that the faces and the eyes of all present are turned towards me: I perceive that you are anxious” (4.1). The repeated “I perceive” fixes the senate’s attention on what Cicero sees in their faces before he says anything about the case.</p><p>The optional figures of repetition differ by where the repeated word stands, and each can be shown in a simple example of our own:</p><p><strong>Epistrophe</strong>: the same word at the end of successive members (“He was born poor, he lived poor, he died poor”).<br><strong>Symploce</strong>: the same words at both the head and the end (“Who planned the theft? The steward. Who hid the money? The steward”).<br><strong>Epizeuxis</strong>: the immediate repetition of the same word (“Work, work, work is all he knows”).<br><strong>Anadiplosis</strong>: the last word of one member taken up at the head of the next (“Fear leads to anger; anger leads to hate”).<br><strong>Conduplicatio</strong>: a key word repeated in successive clauses (“This is a question of duty: duty to our parents, duty to our country”).<br><strong>Polyptoton</strong>: the same root in different forms (“The hunter is hunted”).<br><strong>Epanalepsis</strong>: a member that ends with the word it began with (“Nothing comes of nothing”).<br><strong>Epimone</strong>: a phrase repeated as a refrain, to dwell on one point (“Remember the dead. Whatever else we do tonight, remember the dead”).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “Every street was silent, every window was dark, every door was barred.” Which core figure is this?',
      options:['Chiasmus','Anaphora','Litotes','Apostrophe'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'“Every” stands at the head of each of the three members, which is anaphora. Nothing is crossed in order, nothing is understated, and no one is addressed.' } },
{ id:'balance', src:['cic_yonge','eng_cer','cic_cat','appown'], pids:['cic-marc-1','eng-chesterton-trust','cic-cat4-9','eng-beerbohm-commanded','eng-mfm-rise-fall'], taught:['fig:cic-marc-1:antithesis','fig:eng-chesterton-trust:isocolon','fig:cic-cat4-9:tricolon','fig:eng-beerbohm-commanded:climax','fig:eng-mfm-rise-fall:chiasmus'],
  h:'<p><strong>Figures of balance and rising order</strong></p><p>“Easy come, easy go” and “Waste not, want not” are remembered because their parts match. When clauses have the same shape, the ear compares them, and so the speaker can make the hearers notice a contrast, an equality, or a rise in force without saying that he is doing so.</p><p>The core figures of this group are six:</p><p><strong>Antithesis</strong>: opposed thoughts set in parallel frames. Cicero says that his long silence was “due not to a feeling of fear, but to mingled feelings of grief and of diffidence” (<em>Pro Marcello</em> 1).<br><strong>Isocolon</strong>: members of roughly equal length and shape. “Men trust an ordinary man because they trust themselves. But men trust a great man because they do not trust themselves” (Chesterton, <em>Heretics</em>, 1905).<br><strong>Tricolon</strong>: three coordinated members, often rising. Cicero praises the senate “united in heart and mind and voice” (Fourth Catilinarian 4.9).<br><strong>Climax</strong>: members rising in force, each going beyond the one before. “He did not sue: he invited; he did not invite: he commanded” (Beerbohm, <em>Enoch Soames</em>, 1919).<br><strong>Chiasmus</strong>: a crossed order, in which the second member reverses the first (A B, B A). “Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall” (<em>Measure for Measure</em> II.1).<br><strong>Homoeoteleuton</strong>: like endings on successive clauses, as in a simple example of our own, “He was praised by many and prized by few.”</p><p>One optional figure belongs here as well: <strong>anastrophe</strong>, a word or phrase moved out of its usual order for emphasis (“Strange were the words he spoke”). Several of these figures often appear together, since balanced clauses are usually equal in length and frequently opposed in sense, and sometimes the balance is achieved by what is left out.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'“We make a living by what we get, but we make a life by what we give.” Which core figure does the sentence chiefly show?',
      options:['Antithesis, since the thoughts are opposed in like frames','Apostrophe, since the speaker turns to address the hearers','Hyperbole, since the sentence says more than is true','Asyndeton, since the members have no conjunctions'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'“A living” and “a life,” “what we get” and “what we give” are opposed thoughts set in two clauses of the same shape, which is antithesis. No one absent is addressed, nothing is exaggerated, and the clauses are joined by “but.”' } },
{ id:'omission', src:['cic_cat','appown'], pids:['cic-cat3-10'], taught:['fig:cic-cat3-10:asyndeton'],
  h:'<p><strong>Figures of omission and addition</strong></p><p>“I came, I saw, I conquered” moves fast because it has no “and.” “They ate, and they drank, and they sang, and they slept” moves slowly because it has an “and” before every member. Leaving out or adding the small words that join a series changes the pace of a sentence and the weight of each member.</p><p>The core figure of this group is <strong>asyndeton</strong>: coordination without conjunctions. Cicero tells the people that they have been rescued “without bloodshed, without slaughter, without an army, without a prolonged struggle” (Third Catilinarian 3.10). With no “and” between them, the four phrases come one after another as if they could go on, so that the deliverance seems complete.</p><p>The optional figures of this group are five:</p><p><strong>Polysyndeton</strong>: a conjunction placed between every member of a series, as in the example of the feast above.<br><strong>Ellipsis</strong>: leaving out words that the hearer supplies from the context (“Some chose wealth; others, honor”).<br><strong>Aposiopesis</strong>: breaking off in the middle of a sentence (“If you dare touch her, I will …”).<br><strong>Parenthesis</strong>: a break that inserts a second voice or thought (“The plan (and I say this with regret) has failed”).<br><strong>Hendiadys</strong>: one idea expressed through two coordinated nouns (“with might and main,” for “with all the main force one has”).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “They lost their homes, their fields, their children, their hope.” Which core figure does the series show?',
      options:['Chiasmus','Litotes','Asyndeton','Praeteritio'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'The four members are coordinated without any conjunction, which is asyndeton. The order is not crossed, nothing is understated, and the speaker does not claim to pass over anything.' } },
{ id:'tropes', src:['quint','eng_cer','thuc_crawley','cic_cat','appown'], pids:['eng-richard2-time','eng-hazlitt-nickname','thuc-sthen-1','cic-cat1-7'], taught:['fig:eng-richard2-time:personification','fig:eng-hazlitt-nickname:hyperbole','fig:thuc-sthen-1:irony','fig:cic-cat1-7:personification'],
  h:'<p><strong>Tropes</strong></p><p>We say that “time flies,” that a test was “murder,” that a long wait was “not exactly short,” and, of a man who has just broken a vase, “Well, that was graceful.” In each case a word is not taken in its proper sense, and the hearer understands at once what is meant. We have seen that such a change of meaning is a trope.</p><p>The core tropes are six. Metaphor and the simile, which states a likeness openly with “like” or “as,” have been treated with Aristotle’s doctrine of style. The other four are these:</p><p><strong>Personification</strong>: treating an abstraction as an agent. Shakespeare’s Richard II says, “I wasted time, and now doth time waste me” (<em>Richard II</em> V.5).<br><strong>Hyperbole</strong>: deliberate excess. Hazlitt says, “A nickname is the heaviest stone that the devil can throw at a man” (<em>On Nicknames</em>, 1818).<br><strong>Litotes</strong>: understatement by denying the contrary, as in a simple example of our own, “She is no stranger to hard work,” which means that she has worked very hard.<br><strong>Irony</strong>: saying less, or the opposite, to mean more. The Spartan Sthenelaidas begins, “The long speech of the Athenians I do not pretend to understand” (Thucydides 1.86); he understands it well enough, and he means that it was long and empty.</p><p>Two optional tropes belong with them. <strong>Prosopopoeia</strong> gives a voice to the absent or the dead, or to a thing treated as a person, as when Cicero makes the country speak to Catiline: “Your country, Catilina, pleads with you thus, and appeals with mute eloquence” (First Catilinarian 1.18). An <strong>epithet</strong> is a descriptive word or phrase attached to a name (“Honest Abe,” “the swift Achilles”).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A general reports after a disastrous battle, “Our losses were not light.” Which core trope is this?',
      options:['Hyperbole, since it says far more than is true','Litotes, since it says less than is meant','Apostrophe, since it addresses the dead','Climax, since its members rise in force'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'“Not light” denies the contrary in order to mean “very heavy,” which is litotes, understatement by denying the contrary. Nothing is exaggerated, no one absent is addressed, and there are no rising members.' } },
{ id:'thought', src:['cic_cat','eng_cer','appown'], pids:['cic-cat1-8','eng-henry-liberty-death','cic-cat3-s18','eng-burke-tyrants'], taught:['fig:cic-cat1-8:rhetorical question','fig:eng-henry-liberty-death:apostrophe','fig:cic-cat3-s18:praeteritio','fig:eng-burke-tyrants:sententia'],
  h:'<p><strong>Figures of thought</strong></p><p>A parent who asks a child, “Do you think money grows on trees?” does not want an answer; a mourner who turns from the guests to speak to the dead man in his coffin addresses someone who cannot hear. In such figures the speaker does something with the thought itself, by asking, addressing, or seeming to pass over, and not only with the order of the words.</p><p>The core figures of thought are four:</p><p><strong>Rhetorical question</strong>: a question that is a charge, not a request for information. Cicero asks Catiline, “Why do you wait for them to express their sanction in words, when you can see by their silence the nature of their wishes?” (First Catilinarian 1.20).<br><strong>Apostrophe</strong>: a turn away from the hearers to address someone absent or dead, a god, or a thing treated as a person. Patrick Henry, speaking to the Virginia Convention in 1775, breaks off to address God: “Forbid it, Almighty God!” A plain address to the hearers themselves, such as “men of Athens” or “conscript fathers,” is not apostrophe.<br><strong>Praeteritio</strong>: claiming to pass over what one thereby names. Cicero says, “For to pass over other signs, such as the meteors seen by night in the west and the fiery appearance of the sky” (Third Catilinarian 3.8.18), and so he names them.<br><strong>Sententia</strong>: a general maxim, briefly stated. Burke says, “Kings will be tyrants from policy, when subjects are rebels from principle” (<em>Reflections on the Revolution in France</em>).</p><p>The optional figures of thought are seven:</p><p><strong>Hypophora</strong>: asking a question and then answering it (“What did he gain? Nothing”).<br><strong>Exclamatio</strong>: an open cry of feeling (“O the shame of it!”).<br><strong>Occupatio</strong>: anticipating an objection (“Someone will say that this is costly; I answer that neglect costs more”).<br><strong>Correctio</strong>: taking back a word to put a sharper one (“He was careless, no, reckless”).<br><strong>Hypotyposis</strong>: vivid description that sets a scene before the eyes.<br><strong>Enumeratio</strong>: a numbered or listed unfolding of a matter (“first the cost, then the delay, and last the danger”).<br><strong>Dilemma</strong>: a choice between two alternatives, both unfavorable to the opponent (“Either you knew of the fraud, or you did not; if you knew, you are guilty, and if you did not, you are negligent”).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A speaker says, “I will not mention his debts, his broken promises, or his three lawsuits.” Which core figure is this?',
      options:['Praeteritio','Sententia','Apostrophe','Rhetorical question'],
      correct:0,
      explain:'The speaker claims to pass over the debts, the promises, and the lawsuits, and names them in doing so; that is praeteritio. He states no maxim, addresses no one absent, and asks no question.' } }
]};

DECKS.gorgias = {
  title:'Gorgias · Encomium of Helen', sub:'Persuasion as a drug',
  sources:['gorgias_vh','arist_rhet','arist_freese'],
  panels:[
{ id:'showpiece', src:['gorgias_vh'], pids:['gor-hel-8'],
  h:'<p><strong>A showpiece, not a law-court speech.</strong> The <em>Encomium of Helen</em> is epideictic: it praises a woman the tradition blames, and it displays what logos can do. Gorgias says that speech, small as it is, “can put an end to fear and make vexation vanish; it can inspire exultation and increase compassion” (<em>Helen</em> 8).</p><p>Speech is small, but its effect is not small. Aristotle will classify this as pathos produced by lexis, whereas Gorgias offers it as an account of how the soul is moved.</p>' },
{ id:'four-causes', src:['gorgias_vh'], pids:['gor-hel-6'],
  h:'<p><strong>Four aitiai.</strong> Helen went, and the question is why. Gorgias gives four causes (aitiai), and if any one of them holds, she is to be acquitted:</p><p>first, the disposition of <strong>fortune</strong>, the gods, and necessity;<br>second, <strong>violence</strong>: she was taken;<br>third, <strong>persuasion</strong>: logos deceived her;<br>fourth, <strong>love</strong>.</p><p>“For either by the disposition of fortune and the ratification of the gods and the determination of necessity she did what she did, or by violence confounded, or by persuasion dumbfounded or to Love surrendered.” If logos is a drug, then being persuaded is being acted on, and the accuser has charged the patient.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On Gorgias’s four aitiai, being persuaded by speech is most like',
      options:['Choosing freely, and therefore being guilty','Being acted on, as by a drug, and so not culpable','A logical demonstration from premises that are necessary','A forensic narration of agreed facts'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Helen 8–14 treats logos as a potentate and song as witchery. If persuasion captures the soul, then the one persuaded is not the cause of what he does. Aristotle will not grant that account of agency, and the course asks us to recognize the account rather than to adopt it.' } },
{ id:'witchery', src:['gorgias_vh','arist_rhet'], pids:['gor-hel-10'],
  h:'<p><strong>Witchery.</strong> “Inspired incantations are provocative of charm and revocative of harm. For the power of song in association with the belief of the soul captures and enraptures and translates the soul with witchery” (<em>Helen</em> 10).</p><p>We should hold this beside Aristotle’s cooler sentence, that the hearer is put into a certain frame of mind. The observation is the same, but the account of the soul is different: for Gorgias the hearer is enchanted, whereas for Aristotle he judges, though his judgment is affected by what he feels. Once we have heard the figures in Gorgias, we should ask whether the argument would survive in plain clauses, and that question is the beginning of judgment.</p>' },
{ id:'gorgias-figures', src:['gorgias_vh'], pids:['gor-hel-1','gor-hel-7'], taught:['fig:gor-hel-1:chiasmus','fig:gor-hel-7:homoeoteleuton'],
  h:'<p><strong>Gorgias’s figures</strong></p><p>A jingle in an advertisement is remembered long after the product is forgotten, because its sounds and shapes repeat. Gorgias wrote prose that works in the same way, and the English of the course’s translation is built to keep it: clause answers clause in length and shape, opposites are set side by side, and endings rhyme. Such prose is meant to be heard as figure, and the figures carry much of what it says.</p><p>Four figures do most of the work. First, <strong>antithesis</strong> sets opposed thoughts in parallel frames. Second, <strong>isocolon</strong> makes the members equal in length and shape. Third, <strong>homoeoteleuton</strong> gives the clauses like endings, as when Gorgias says that Helen was “defeated and unlawfully she was treated and to her injustice was meted” (<em>Helen</em> 7). Fourth, <strong>chiasmus</strong> crosses the order of the terms, as in his charge that some men “censure the commendable and commend the censurable” (<em>Helen</em> 1): the verb and its object in the first member are reversed in the second.</p><p>Aristotle names the same devices in his account of the periodic style: opposed clauses, equality of clauses, and like endings (<em>Rhetoric</em> III.9.7–9). What distinguishes Gorgias is not that he uses them, since every orator does, but that he uses them almost everywhere, so that the figure becomes the substance of the speech. That is why the <em>Helen</em> is the clearest place to hear these figures, and also why it raises the question whether its argument would survive without them.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'What chiefly distinguishes Gorgias’s use of the figures, on the account given here?',
      options:['He uses figures that no other orator before or after him uses','He uses them nearly everywhere, so the figure is the substance','He avoids antithesis and uses only like endings','He uses figures only in the peroration'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Every orator uses antithesis, equal members, and like endings, and Aristotle names them in his account of the period. Gorgias is distinguished by using them almost everywhere, so that the figure carries much of what the speech says.' } }
]};

DECKS.antiphon = {
  title:'Antiphon · Second Tetralogy', sub:'A forensic school-piece',
  sources:['antiphon','arist_freese','arist_rhet'],
  panels:[
{ id:'the-case', src:['antiphon'], pids:['ant-3.1.1','ant-3.2.1','ant-3.3.1','ant-3.4.1'],
  h:'<p><strong>The case</strong></p><p>During javelin practice a boy runs out across the field, is struck in the side, and dies on the spot. The facts are agreed. The prosecutor, the dead boy’s father, charges the young man who threw the javelin, not with intentional but with <em>unintentional</em> homicide. There are four speeches, two on each side:</p><p><strong>3.1</strong>: the prosecution’s first speech.<br><strong>3.2</strong>: the defense’s first speech.<br><strong>3.3</strong>: the prosecution’s second speech.<br><strong>3.4</strong>: the defense’s second speech.</p><p>So the speeches numbered 3.1 and 3.3 are the prosecution, and 3.2 and 3.4 are the defense. That is what a tetralogy is: a set of four speeches in the shape of a trial, written as a school exercise rather than for a real court.</p><p>What is on trial is <strong>cause</strong> and <strong>error</strong> (<span class="lat">hamartia</span>, literally a missing of the mark). So the question is who missed: the thrower, whose javelin flew true toward the target, or the boy who ran into its path? Pollution (<span class="lat">miasma</span>), the religious stain of an unpunished killing, is said to hang over the city until a verdict names the one responsible. Several pairs of terms run through the speeches: deeds (<span class="lat">erga</span>) against words (<span class="lat">logoi</span>), and opinion (<span class="lat">doxa</span>) against truth (<span class="lat">aletheia</span>). The defense is spoken by the father of the young man who threw the javelin, and he presents himself as <span class="lat">apragmon</span>, a quiet citizen who minds his own business and has been forced into court by misfortune.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Which speeches of the Second Tetralogy belong to the defense?',
      options:['3.1 and 3.3','3.2 and 3.4','3.1 and 3.2','3.3 and 3.4'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'The speeches alternate, beginning with the prosecution: 3.1 and 3.3 are the prosecution’s two speeches, and 3.2 and 3.4 are the defense’s.' } },
{ id:'how-to-read', src:['antiphon','arist_freese','arist_rhet'],
  h:'<p><strong>How to read it</strong></p><p>We have seen that the facts of the throw are agreed, so every speech after the first redescribes the same event. Of each passage we can therefore ask three questions: which side is speaking, which means of persuasion it chiefly uses, and from which topic its argument is drawn.</p><p>The exercise on this case uses ten topic labels of its own, written for the javelin case. Each is explained here, with Aristotle’s name for the topic where his list has one:</p><p><strong>From the laws</strong> (facts agreed, verdict from law): since the facts are not disputed, the verdict is to be given by the laws. Aristotle counts laws among the inartistic proofs (<em>Rhetoric</em> I.15), not among the topics.<br><strong>From character</strong> (the apragmon forced into court): the speaker presents himself as a quiet man who would not be in court unless compelled. This is ethos, not one of Aristotle’s topics.<br><strong>From cause</strong> (the running-out, not the throw): the death is assigned to the act that caused it. Aristotle’s topic of <strong>cause</strong>.<br><strong>From the name of the act</strong> (whose hamartia?): the dispute is what the act should be called, and whose error it was. It is closest to Aristotle’s topic of <strong>definition</strong>.<br><strong>From consequences</strong> (childlessness): the speaker argues from what follows for him if the verdict goes against him. Aristotle’s topic of <strong>consequence</strong>.<br><strong>From refutation of the defense’s descriptions</strong>: the prosecution answers the way the other side has described the event. This is refutation, which Aristotle treats as part of the argument; it is not one of his named topics.<br><strong>From more and less</strong> (who missed the target): the argument asks which of the two is more likely to have missed. Aristotle’s topic of <strong>more and less</strong>.<br><strong>From the city’s pollution if the killer walks</strong>: the speaker argues from what will follow for the whole city, under the religious law of pollution, if no one is punished. In Aristotle’s terms this too is an argument from <strong>consequence</strong>, drawn from a belief about pollution.<br><strong>From character again, and from misfortune</strong>: the defense returns to its own character and to the misfortune that has brought it to court. Again ethos, not a named topic.<br><strong>From cause restated</strong> (the boy’s own error): the cause is assigned again, to the boy who ran into the path. Aristotle’s topic of <strong>cause</strong>.</p><p>So two labels name Aristotle’s cause, two his consequence, one his more and less, and one is close to his definition, whereas the labels from character, from the laws, and from refutation name other parts of the art. These are not Ciceronian orations, but a drill in invention for a case whose narration is finished at the start.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In the Antiphon set, the label “from more and less (who missed the target)” corresponds to which of Aristotle’s topics?',
      options:['Cause','Consequence','More and less','Previous judgment'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'The label asks which of the two is more likely to have missed, which is Aristotle’s topic of more and less. Cause assigns the effect to what produced it, consequence argues from what follows, and previous judgment appeals to a decision already made.' } }
]};

DECKS.augustine = {
  title:'Augustine · The Christian Orator', sub:'Docere, delectare, flectere',
  sources:['ddc','cic_orat'],
  panels:[
{ id:'offices', src:['ddc','cic_orat'], pids:['aug-ddc-2','aug-ddc-12','aug-ddc-17'],
  h:'<p><strong>Three offices, kept</strong></p><p>A good teacher does three things at once. He explains the lesson so that it is understood; he makes it interesting, so that the students want to listen; and, when it matters, he moves them to act on what they have learned. A teacher who only explains may be right and still be ignored, and one who only entertains may be enjoyed and teach nothing.</p><p>St. Augustine, in the fourth book of <em>On Christian Doctrine</em>, asks whether a Christian preacher should use the art of rhetoric at all, since the art can serve falsehood. He answers that it must: “Now, the art of rhetoric being available for the enforcing either of truth or falsehood, who will dare to say that truth in the person of its defenders is to take its stand unarmed against falsehood?” (IV.2.3). He then keeps Cicero’s account of the orator’s three offices: “a great orator has truly said that an eloquent man must speak so as to teach, to delight, and to persuade. Then he adds: To teach is a necessity, to delight is a beauty, to persuade is a triumph” (IV.12.27). The Latin has <span class="lat">ut doceat, ut delectet, ut flectat</span>, and the course names the three offices with the words Augustine quotes from Cicero (<i>Orator</i> 69):</p><p><strong>To teach</strong> (<span class="lat">docere</span>).<br><strong>To delight</strong> (<span class="lat">delectare</span>).<br><strong>To move</strong> (<span class="lat">flectere</span>; Shaw’s translation has “to persuade”).</p><p>Teaching is the necessary office, because it “depends on what we say; the other two on the way we say it.”</p><p>Three styles answer to the three offices, again following Cicero: the speaker should say “little things in a subdued style, moderate things in a temperate style, and great things in a majestic style” (IV.17.34). The course calls them the <strong>subdued</strong> (<span class="lat">summissum</span>), the <strong>temperate</strong> (<span class="lat">temperatum</span>), and the <strong>grand</strong> (<span class="lat">grande</span>) style; Shaw’s “majestic” is the grand style. The subdued style serves teaching, the temperate delighting, and the grand moving. What changes for the Christian orator is the end: he serves a truth already given in Scripture, and so eloquence is not refused but subordinated. But Cicero’s rule ties the style to the size of the subject, and Augustine corrects it.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'Why, on Augustine’s account, should a defender of the truth learn rhetoric?',
      options:['Because winning a case is the end of the art, whatever the cause','Because truth should not stand unarmed against falsehood','Because the Scriptures command preachers to study the pagan orators','Because rhetoric is good in itself, whatever cause it serves'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Augustine says that since the art can enforce either truth or falsehood, the defenders of truth must not be left unarmed while its opponents use the art. He does not make winning the end, and he does not say that the art is good whatever cause it serves.' } },
{ id:'purpose', src:['ddc'], pids:['aug-ddc-19-38','aug-ddc-20-42-style'],
  h:'<p><strong>The style follows the purpose</strong></p><p>A doctor speaks of a serious illness quietly when he explains it to a medical student, calmly when he praises a patient who has kept to the treatment, and with force when he must persuade an unwilling patient to have the operation that will save his life. The illness is equally serious in each case, but the purpose of the speech changes, and so does the way he speaks.</p><p>Augustine applies the same rule to preaching. For the Christian teacher every subject is great, since it concerns salvation, so Cicero’s rule that great things call for the grand style would require him to speak always in the grand style. Augustine answers that the style follows what the speaker must do at that moment, not the size of the subject: the teacher “ought not always to be speaking of them in a majestic tone, but in a subdued tone when he is teaching, temperately when he is giving praise or blame. When, however, something is to be done, and we are speaking to those who ought, but are not willing, to do it, then great matters must be spoken of with power, and in a manner calculated to sway the mind” (IV.19.38). He then shows from the writings of St. Ambrose, as well as from St. Paul, that one great subject may be treated in each of the three styles according to the purpose of the passage (IV.20–21).</p><p>He also explains what distinguishes the grand style from the temperate. It is not more ornament: “The majestic style of speech differs from the temperate style just spoken of, chiefly in that it is not so much decked out with verbal ornaments as exalted into vehemence by mental emotion” (IV.20.42). The grand style uses ornaments when they come, but it does not seek them. So the test of the grand style is what it does to the hearer.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'A preacher must speak of the same great doctrine three times: to explain it to beginners, to praise those who live by it, and to move the unwilling to accept it. On Augustine’s account, which styles should he use?',
      options:['The grand style each time, because the doctrine is great','The subdued, the temperate, and the grand, in that order','The subdued style each time, because doctrine needs proof','The temperate style each time, because it delights the hearer'],
      correct:1,
      explain:'Augustine says that the style follows the purpose, not the size of the subject: subdued for teaching, temperate for praise or blame, and grand when the unwilling must be moved to act. To use the grand style every time would be to follow the size of the subject.' } },
{ id:'tears', src:['ddc'], pids:['aug-ddc-24','aug-ddc-24-54','aug-ddc-25-55'],
  h:'<p><strong>Tears, not applause</strong></p><p>An audience may cheer a speaker and go home unchanged; another may sit in silence and leave determined to change its life. Applause shows that the hearers were pleased, but it does not show that they were moved to act.</p><p>Augustine tells of preaching at Caesarea in Mauretania against the yearly civil brawl called the Caterva, in which “neighbors, brothers, fathers and sons even, who, divided into two factions and armed with stones, fought annually at a certain season of the year for several days continuously” (IV.24.53). The people applauded, but, he says, “it was not, however, when I heard their applause, but when I saw their tears, that I thought I had produced an effect”; and the custom was not attempted again for eight years or more. “The majestic style, on the other hand, frequently silences the audience by its impressiveness, but calls forth their tears.” So the test of the grand style is not the noise in the room but whether the hearer is changed.</p><p>Augustine sets out what change each style makes (IV.24–25). The subdued style changes many, but “it was to teach them what they were ignorant of, or to persuade them of what they thought incredible, not to make them do what they knew they ought to do but were unwilling to do” (IV.24.54). In his summary, “in the subdued style, he persuades his hearers that what he says is true; in the majestic style, he persuades them to do what they are aware they ought to do, but do not; in the temperate style, he persuades them that his speech is elegant and ornate” (IV.25.55). Each style persuades, then, but of something different. A whole discourse, however, seldom keeps to one style.</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'On Augustine’s account, of what does the temperate style persuade its hearers?',
      options:['That what the speaker says is true','To do what they know they ought to do, but do not','That the speaker’s speech is elegant and ornate','That they should applaud and then leave unchanged'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Augustine says that in the temperate style the speaker persuades his hearers that his speech is elegant and ornate. The subdued style persuades them that what he says is true, and the grand style persuades them to do what they know they ought to do.' } },
{ id:'mixing-wisdom', src:['ddc'], pids:['aug-ddc-22-51','aug-ddc-23-52','aug-ddc-7-12','aug-ddc-28-61'],
  h:'<p><strong>Mixing the styles; wisdom before eloquence</strong></p><p>A lecture given entirely in one tone, however fine, puts the hearers to sleep, whereas a good teacher moves from explanation to an example, to a moment of feeling, and back again. And a speaker who speaks beautifully about what is false does more harm than one who speaks plainly about what is true.</p><p>Augustine teaches both points. First, the styles should be mixed: “For when we keep monotonously to one style, we fail to retain the hearer’s attention; but when we pass from one style to another, the discourse goes off more gracefully, even though it extend to greater length” (IV.22.51). Second, a hard question needs the subdued style whatever the style around it: “whatever may be the style of the speech or writing, when knotty questions turn up for solution, accuracy of distinction is required, and this naturally demands the subdued style” (IV.23.52).</p><p>Third, and most important, wisdom comes before eloquence. Augustine says of St. Paul that “wisdom is his guide, eloquence his attendant; he follows the first, the second follows him, and yet he does not spurn it when it comes after him” (IV.7.12). And he warns that “we must beware of the man who abounds in eloquent nonsense” (IV.5.7). So the Christian orator learns eloquence, but he learns it as the servant of what is true, and when he cannot have both, he should prefer to speak wisely without eloquence rather than eloquently without wisdom (IV.28.61).</p>',
  q:{ prompt:'In the middle of a sermon in the grand style, a preacher must explain a hard distinction about the sacraments. On Augustine’s account, which style does that explanation need?',
      options:['The grand style, since the sermon began in it','The temperate style, since ornament makes it easier','The subdued style, since it needs accuracy of distinction','No style at all, since doctrine needs no eloquence'],
      correct:2,
      explain:'Augustine says that whatever the style of the speech, a knotty question requires accuracy of distinction, and this demands the subdued style. The style should change with the task, not stay with the style in which the sermon began.' } }
]};

/* Room guides R.1–R.3 (RHETORICA-CARD-PLAN.md, sections 3.9 and 5): one card at the head of each whole-speech room.
   The Gettysburg Address is the parallel to Pericles, so its room opens with R.1. A link with data-room opens that room. */
const ROOM_GUIDES = {
  pericles:{ id:'R.1', src:['thuc_crawley','lincoln_ev'],
    pids:['thuc-fun-1','thuc-per-4','thuc-per-2','thuc-fun-41','thuc-fun-42','thuc-fun-44'],
    h:'<p><strong>Pericles’ Funeral Oration</strong></p><p>When a city buries those who died in its defense, someone is usually asked to speak over them. He is not arguing for a policy or defending anyone in a trial; he has to honor the dead in words that their families and fellow citizens can accept as true, and he has to say something to the living who remain. So the speech is praise, of the kind the tradition calls epideictic, and its hearers judge the dead, the city, and the speaker himself.</p><p>Athens made such a speech part of its law. Thucydides, the Athenian historian of the war between Athens and Sparta (late fifth century BC), reports that the bones of those who fell were buried at the public cost, and that a man chosen by the city for his judgment and reputation then spoke over them (2.34). At the end of the first year of the war (431–430 BC) the speaker was Pericles, the leading statesman of Athens. He begins by questioning the custom, since “I should have thought that the worth which had displayed itself in deeds would be sufficiently rewarded by honours also shown by deeds”; but, he says, “since our ancestors have stamped this custom with their approval, it becomes my duty to obey the law” (2.35).</p><p>The speech is arranged in this order:</p><p>Pericles begins with the custom itself and says how hard it is to praise the dead well (2.35).<br>He then praises the ancestors, who “handed it down free to the present time by their valour,” and the fathers who added the empire (2.36).<br>He describes the city, its constitution, whose administration “favours the many instead of the few” (2.37), and its way of life (2.37–41).<br>He turns to the dead, whose death is the test of their worth (2.42), and he urges the survivors to match their resolution (2.43).<br>He consoles the parents, the sons and brothers, and the widows (2.44–45), and then he closes briefly (2.46).</p><p>In a first reading two things deserve attention. First, the long praise of the city is the proof of the praise of the men, as Pericles himself says: he has dwelt on the character of the country so “that the panegyric of the men over whom I am now speaking might be by definite proofs established,” because “the Athens that I have celebrated is only what the heroism of these and their like have made her” (2.42). Second, the speech turns from praise to the living: “Comfort, therefore, not condolence, is what I have to offer to the parents of the dead who may be here” (2.44). The consolation does not deny the loss; it gives the parents reasons on which to bear it.</p><p>Abraham Lincoln, President of the United States, spoke at the dedication of the national cemetery at Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, on November 19, 1863, during the American Civil War. <a href="#" class="room-link" data-room="gettysburg">The Gettysburg Address</a> is a speech of the same kind, and the course sets it beside <a href="#" class="room-link" data-room="pericles">Pericles’ speech</a> as a parallel. It too begins from the fathers: “Fourscore and seven years ago our fathers brought forth upon this continent a new nation” (1). It too says that deeds honor the dead more than words can, since “The brave men, living and dead, who struggled here, have consecrated it far above our power to add or detract” (3). And it too ends with the task of the living.</p>' },
  catilinarian1:{ id:'R.2', src:['cic_cat'],
    pids:['cic-cat1-1','cic-cat1-2','cic-cat1-4','cic-cat1-6','cic-cat1-7','cic-cat1-13'],
    h:'<p><strong>The First Catilinarian</strong></p><p>A man accused of plotting against his community does not usually sit among the people he is accused of plotting against while the charge is made. In the First Catilinarian he does. Marcus Tullius Cicero (106–43 BC), the Roman orator and statesman, was one of the two consuls (the chief magistrates of Rome) in 63 BC. In November of that year he spoke in the senate against Lucius Sergius Catilina (Catiline), a senator who, Cicero says, had planned the burning of the city and the murder of the consul. Catiline was present: “the criminal still lives. Lives? Yes, lives; and even comes down to the senate, takes part in the public deliberations” (1.2).</p><p>The speech is forensic in its charge, because it accuses Catiline of what he has planned and done, and Cicero tells him that “your conspiracy is even now fully known and detected by all who are here assembled” (1.1). But it is deliberative in what it asks, because no court is sitting and no verdict is wanted. Cicero wants Catiline to leave Rome: “Quit Rome at last and soon; the city gates are open; depart at once” (1.10). And he argues that this is the safer course for the city, since “if I order you to be executed, there will still remain the dregs of your conspiracy to trouble the state,” but if Catiline departs, “the city will be emptied of your dangerous associates” (1.12).</p><p>We have traced the six offices of the oration in this speech. Four of the six appear plainly in the full text, in this order:</p><p>The exordium is a run of questions put to Catiline himself, “How much further, Catilina, will you carry your abuse of our forbearance?” (1.1).<br>The narration tells the night at Laeca’s house in order (1.9), and the same night is then pressed as proof, when Cicero tells how he kept the assassins from his door (1.10).<br>In the refutation, Cicero answers the charge that his country might bring against him for letting Catiline go free (1.27–29).<br>The peroration is a prayer that Jupiter will keep Catiline and his associates from the city and punish them (1.33).</p><p>Two features of the speech deserve attention in a first reading. First, Cicero addresses Catiline, but he is speaking to the senate, whose fear and resolve he wants to move; so he can point to their silence as an answer: “Their silence denotes consent” (1.20). Second, he makes Rome herself speak: “Your country, Catilina, pleads with you thus, and appeals with mute eloquence” (1.18). The figure is personification: the country is given a voice, and its speech gathers the charges against Catiline.</p>' },
  archias:{ id:'R.3', src:['cic_yonge'],
    pids:['cic-arch-1','cic-arch-8','cic-arch-16','cic-arch-19','cic-arch-29'],
    h:'<p><strong>For Archias</strong></p><p>When someone’s right to remain in a community is challenged, the legal question is usually narrow: was the name enrolled, and was the paper filed in time? But those who decide are also likely to ask whether the person is worth keeping, even though that is not the question before them. A speaker who can show both that the law is satisfied and that the community would lose something by losing him has given them every reason to decide as he asks.</p><p>Cicero (106–43 BC) defended Aulus Licinius Archias, a Greek poet born at Antioch, who had lived at Rome for many years in the house of the Luculli, against the charge that he was not a Roman citizen. So the case stands on the issue of fact, that is, whether Archias was a citizen. Cicero settles it quickly. Archias had been enrolled as a citizen of Heraclea and had returned himself to the praetor under the law of Silvanus and Carbo (7); Lucius Lucullus is present to testify, “who will tell you not that he thinks it, but that he knows it” (8); and if his name is not in the census returns, Cicero explains why, and adds that the census does not in any case confirm the right of citizenship (11). Cicero then says, “If we have nothing else to speak about except the rights of citizenship and the law, I need say no more. The cause is over” (8).</p><p>The rest of the speech, from section 12 nearly to the end, is a long praise of letters. Cicero answers the prosecutor’s question, “You ask us, O Gratius, why we are so exceedingly attached to this man” (12): these studies “are the food of youth, the delight of old age” (16); the name of poet should be “holy in your eyes” (19); and the hope of fame is what moves good men to great labors (29). This praise does not prove that Archias is a citizen. Its work is to make the jurors want to keep him, and Cicero states that aim near the beginning, that Archias “is worthy, even if he were not one, of being now made a citizen” (4).</p><p>Cicero knows that this is not the usual manner of a court, and he asks the judges’ leave “to employ a new and unusual style of oratory” (3). So a first reading can attend to the proportion of the speech: a short proof on the issue, and a long praise that serves the verdict, which the peroration then asks for: “Preserve then, O judges, a man of such virtue as that of Archias” (31).</p>' }
};
